import{_ as e,o,c as n,a2 as a}from"./chunks/framework.Dkj3JE0_.js";const c=JSON.parse('{"title":"Japanese Grammar Foundations","description":"","frontmatter":{"title":"Japanese Grammar Foundations"},"headers":[],"relativePath":"blogs/JLPT-N2/grammars.md","filePath":"blogs/JLPT-N2/grammars.md"}'),s={name:"blogs/JLPT-N2/grammars.md"};function r(i,t,l,g,h,d){return o(),n("div",null,[...t[0]||(t[0]=[a('<h1 id="japanese-grammar-foundations" tabindex="-1">Japanese Grammar Foundations <a class="header-anchor" href="#japanese-grammar-foundations" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Japanese Grammar Foundations&quot;">​</a></h1><p>These basic patterns are useful to review before studying N2 grammar. In the patterns below, <strong>N</strong> means a noun, and <strong>A/B</strong> stand for words or phrases in the sentence.</p><h2 id="a-は-b-です" tabindex="-1">A は B です <a class="header-anchor" href="#a-は-b-です" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;A は B です&quot;">​</a></h2><p><strong>Rule:</strong> は (pronounced <em>wa</em>) marks the topic: “as for A.” です makes a noun or な-adjective predicate polite. The topic is not always the grammatical subject.</p><ul><li>私は留学生です。 — I am an international student.</li><li>あの方は先生です。 — That person is a teacher.</li></ul><p><strong>Negative:</strong> A は B ではありません (or the conversational B じゃありません).</p><ul><li>私は先生ではありません。 — I am not a teacher.</li></ul><p><strong>Question:</strong> Add か to the end of a polite sentence. In writing, a question mark is optional.</p><ul><li>あの方は先生ですか。 — Is that person a teacher?</li></ul><p><strong>Connecting predicates:</strong> B で connects a noun or な-adjective predicate to another predicate. This で is a connective form of the copula, not the particle for the location of an action.</p><ul><li>田中さんは先生で、作家です。 — Tanaka is a teacher and a writer.</li></ul><h2 id="demonstratives" tabindex="-1">Demonstratives <a class="header-anchor" href="#demonstratives" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Demonstratives&quot;">​</a></h2><p><strong>Rule:</strong> The first syllable shows where the referent is relative to the speakers: <strong>こ</strong> near the speaker, <strong>そ</strong> near the listener or something already mentioned, <strong>あ</strong> away from both, and <strong>ど</strong> for a question. In conversation, そ and あ can also refer to familiar or remembered things; physical distance is only the starting point.</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Position</th><th>Thing</th><th>Place</th><th>Direction / polite person</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Near speaker</td><td>これ</td><td>ここ</td><td>こちら</td></tr><tr><td>Near listener</td><td>それ</td><td>そこ</td><td>そちら</td></tr><tr><td>Away from both</td><td>あれ</td><td>あそこ</td><td>あちら</td></tr><tr><td>Question</td><td>どれ</td><td>どこ</td><td>どちら</td></tr></tbody></table><ul><li>これは私の本です。 — This is my book.</li><li>駅はどちらですか。 — Which way is the station? (polite)</li></ul><p>こちら／そちら／あちら／どちら can also politely refer to a person or choice, depending on context.</p><h2 id="n1-に-n2-が-あります-います" tabindex="-1">N1 に N2 が あります／います <a class="header-anchor" href="#n1-に-n2-が-あります-います" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;N1 に N2 が あります／います&quot;">​</a></h2><p><strong>Rule:</strong> This pattern introduces N2 as existing at N1. に marks the location; が marks what exists. Use <strong>あります</strong> for things and plants, and <strong>います</strong> for people and animals.</p><ul><li>机の上に本があります。 — There is a book on the desk.</li><li>教室に学生がいます。 — There are students in the classroom.</li></ul><p>For possession, the location can be a person: 私には兄がいます。 — I have an older brother (literally, “As for me, an older brother exists”).</p><h2 id="n1-には-n2-が-あります-います" tabindex="-1">N1 には N2 が あります／います <a class="header-anchor" href="#n1-には-n2-が-あります-います" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;N1 には N2 が あります／います&quot;">​</a></h2><p><strong>Rule:</strong> には combines the location marker に with the topic or contrast marker は. It makes N1 the frame of the statement and can imply a contrast with other places or people. Use があります／います according to N2.</p><ul><li>この町には大きな公園があります。 — This town has a large park.</li><li>私のクラスには留学生がいます。 — There are international students in my class.</li></ul><p>Compare <strong>教室に学生がいます</strong> (“There are students in the classroom”) with <strong>教室には学生がいます</strong> (“In the classroom, there are students”), which puts more focus on the classroom.</p><h2 id="n1-は-n2-に-あります-います" tabindex="-1">N1 は N2 に あります／います <a class="header-anchor" href="#n1-は-n2-に-あります-います" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;N1 は N2 に あります／います&quot;">​</a></h2><p><strong>Rule:</strong> Use this order when N1 is already known and you want to say <strong>where</strong> it is. は marks the known topic; に marks its location. Choose あります／います according to N1.</p><ul><li>本は机の上にあります。 — The book is on the desk.</li><li>先生は職員室にいます。 — The teacher is in the staff room.</li></ul><p>Compare <strong>机の上に本があります</strong> (“There is a book on the desk”), which introduces the book, with <strong>本は机の上にあります</strong> (“The book is on the desk”), which gives the location of a known book.</p><h2 id="past-tense-noun-predicates" tabindex="-1">Past tense: noun predicates <a class="header-anchor" href="#past-tense-noun-predicates" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Past tense: noun predicates&quot;">​</a></h2><p><strong>Rule:</strong> For a polite noun sentence, change です to <strong>でした</strong>. The polite negative past is <strong>ではありませんでした</strong> (or conversational <strong>じゃありませんでした</strong>). These forms also work with な-adjective predicates.</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Meaning</th><th>Pattern</th><th>Example</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Past affirmative</td><td>A は B でした</td><td>昨日は休みでした。 — Yesterday was a day off.</td></tr><tr><td>Past negative</td><td>A は B ではありませんでした</td><td>昨日は休みではありませんでした。 — Yesterday was not a day off.</td></tr></tbody></table><p>The plain forms are <strong>だった</strong> and <strong>ではなかった</strong> (or <strong>じゃなかった</strong>): 昨日は休みだった。 — Yesterday was a day off.</p><h2 id="past-tense-verb-predicates" tabindex="-1">Past tense: verb predicates <a class="header-anchor" href="#past-tense-verb-predicates" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Past tense: verb predicates&quot;">​</a></h2><p><strong>Rule:</strong> A polite verb changes from <strong>～ます</strong> to <strong>～ました</strong> in the affirmative past, and from <strong>～ません</strong> to <strong>～ませんでした</strong> in the negative past. The plain past form depends on the verb group, so it cannot be made by simply replacing ます with た.</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Meaning</th><th>Polite</th><th>Plain</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Past affirmative</td><td>昨日、映画を見ました。 — I watched a film yesterday.</td><td>昨日、映画を見た。</td></tr><tr><td>Past negative</td><td>昨日、映画を見ませんでした。 — I did not watch a film yesterday.</td><td>昨日、映画を見なかった。</td></tr></tbody></table><p>For example, 行きます → 行きました → 行った, while 食べます → 食べました → 食べた.</p><h2 id="question-words-with-が" tabindex="-1">Question words with が <a class="header-anchor" href="#question-words-with-が" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Question words with が&quot;">​</a></h2><p><strong>Rule:</strong> When a question word asks <strong>who or what the subject is</strong>, mark it with が: 誰が／何が. The answer commonly uses が to identify that subject. Use は instead if the answer treats the person or thing as an established topic or makes a contrast; “a が question must be answered with が” is too absolute.</p><ul><li>誰が来ましたか。 — Who came?</li><li>田中さんが来ました。 — Tanaka came.</li><li>何がありますか。 — What is there?</li><li>机の上に本があります。 — There is a book on the desk.</li></ul><h2 id="この-その-あの-どの" tabindex="-1">この／その／あの／どの <a class="header-anchor" href="#この-その-あの-どの" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;この／その／あの／どの&quot;">​</a></h2><p><strong>Rule:</strong> These are <strong>連体詞</strong> (words that modify a noun directly). They must be followed by a noun: この本 (“this book”), その本 (“that book near you / the book mentioned”), あの本 (“that book over there”), どの本 (“which book”). To refer to a thing without naming the noun, use これ／それ／あれ／どれ instead.</p><ul><li>この本は面白いです。 — This book is interesting.</li><li>これは面白いです。 — This is interesting.</li><li>どの本があなたのですか。 — Which book is yours?</li><li>どれがあなたのですか。 — Which one is yours?</li></ul><h2 id="n-を-n-をください" tabindex="-1">N を / N をください <a class="header-anchor" href="#n-を-n-をください" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;N を / N をください&quot;">​</a></h2><p><strong>Rule:</strong> The particle <strong>を</strong> follows a noun and marks the <strong>direct object</strong>. Japanese is an SOV language, so the verb usually comes at the end of the sentence; the object appears earlier and needs を to mark it.</p><p><strong>N をください:</strong> ください is the imperative of くださる (“to give”). <strong>N をください</strong> means “please give me …” and is common when shopping or ordering.</p><ul><li>水族館の入場券をください。 — Please give me an aquarium admission ticket.</li><li>チーズバーガーセットをください。 — Please give me a cheeseburger set.</li><li>ビールをください。 — Please give me a beer.</li></ul><p><strong>も:</strong> To say “also / too,” use <strong>も</strong>. も and を do not stack — も <strong>replaces</strong> を.</p><ul><li>それから、ホットコーヒーもください。 — And then, please give me a hot coffee too.</li></ul><h2 id="n-を-quantity-ください" tabindex="-1">N を (quantity) ください <a class="header-anchor" href="#n-を-quantity-ください" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;N を (quantity) ください&quot;">​</a></h2><p><strong>Rule:</strong> To say how many you want, put the <strong>quantifier</strong> (数量詞) before ください: <strong>N を [quantifier] ください</strong>. Quantifiers generally sit before the verb in Japanese.</p><ul><li>この水族館の入場券を3枚ください。 — Please give me three admission tickets for this aquarium. / はい、3000円です。 — Sure, that’ll be 3000 yen.</li><li>チーズバーガーセットを一つください。 — Please give me one cheeseburger set.</li><li>あのう、これを2本ください。 — Um, please give me two of these.</li><li>シャツを3枚ください。 — Please give me three shirts.</li></ul><p>Quantifiers also come before other verbs: 兄が2人います。 — I have two older brothers. / スマホが2台あります。 — There are two smartphones.</p><p>Counters used here: <strong>枚</strong> (flat objects), <strong>一つ</strong> (general), <strong>本</strong> (long/cylindrical), <strong>人</strong> (people), <strong>台</strong> (machines).</p><h2 id="n-をお願いします-n-でお願いします" tabindex="-1">N をお願いします / N でお願いします <a class="header-anchor" href="#n-をお願いします-n-でお願いします" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;N をお願いします / N でお願いします&quot;">​</a></h2><p><strong>Rule:</strong> <strong>お願いします</strong> is a polite request (“please”). It can replace <strong>ください</strong> in many ordering contexts.</p><ul><li>ホットコーヒーをお願いします。 — A hot coffee, please.</li><li>これを４つお願いします。 — Four of these, please.</li></ul><p><strong>N でお願いします:</strong> The particle <strong>で</strong> marks the <strong>means / method / chosen option</strong>. Use it when specifying size, payment, channel, and so on — especially when the item itself is already clear in context.</p><ul><li>Sサイズでお願いします。 — Small, please. (natural when the clerk already asked about size)</li><li>Sサイズのコーヒーをお願いします。 — A small coffee, please. (names the item)</li><li>アリペイでお願いします。 — Alipay, please. (at payment)</li><li>Eメールでお願いします。 — By email, please. (when asking how to contact)</li></ul><h2 id="n-でございます" tabindex="-1">N でございます <a class="header-anchor" href="#n-でございます" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;N でございます&quot;">​</a></h2><p><strong>Rule:</strong> <strong>でございます</strong> is a more polite / formal equivalent of <strong>です</strong> (丁寧語). It is common in service speech, speeches, and other formal settings. It is typically used for the speaker, the speaker’s in-group, or things belonging to them — not usually for the respected listener or the listener’s things.</p><ul><li>エレベーターはこちらでございます。 — The elevator is this way.</li><li>私が担当者でございます。 — I am the person in charge.</li><li>部長の田中でございます。 — This is Department Manager Tanaka. <em>(colleague introducing their own company’s manager to a client)</em></li></ul><p>Dialogue:</p><ul><li>A <em>(outsider)</em>: 田中部長は、今日、お休みですか。 — Is Manager Tanaka off today?</li><li>B <em>(colleague)</em>: はい、休みでございます。 — Yes, he is off today.</li></ul><h2 id="どうなさいますか-はどうですか" tabindex="-1">どうなさいますか / ～はどうですか <a class="header-anchor" href="#どうなさいますか-はどうですか" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;どうなさいますか / ～はどうですか&quot;">​</a></h2><p><strong>Rule:</strong> <strong>どう</strong> means “how / what (choice).” <strong>なさいます</strong> is the honorific form of <strong>する</strong>, so <strong>どうなさいますか</strong> is a very polite “What would you like?” / “How would you like it?” — common in service speech.</p><ul><li>A: ホットコーヒーのサイズはどうなさいますか。 — What size hot coffee would you like?</li><li>B: Lサイズでお願いします。 — Large, please.</li></ul><p><strong>～はどうですか:</strong> When <strong>どう</strong> is the predicate with <strong>ですか</strong>, <strong>～はどうですか</strong> asks for an opinion or how something is going.</p><ul><li>このコーヒーはどうですか。 — How is this coffee?</li><li>清華大学はどうですか。 — How is Tsinghua University?</li><li>最近はどうですか。 — How have you been lately?</li></ul><h2 id="adjective-predicates-polite-non-past" tabindex="-1">Adjective predicates (polite non-past) <a class="header-anchor" href="#adjective-predicates-polite-non-past" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Adjective predicates (polite non-past)&quot;">​</a></h2><p><strong>Rule:</strong> Adjectival predicates describe a <strong>state</strong> or a <strong>constant property</strong>. Japanese adjectives fall into two types: <strong>Type I</strong> (い-adjectives, ending in <strong>い</strong>) and <strong>Type II</strong> (な-adjectives / adjectival verbs).</p><p><strong>Polite non-past (敬体):</strong></p><ol><li><strong>Affirmative:</strong> Type I → <strong>A₁ + です</strong>; Type II → <strong>A₂ + です</strong>.</li><li><strong>Negative:</strong> Type I → drop <strong>い</strong>, then <strong>く + ありません</strong>; Type II → <strong>A₂ + ではありません</strong> (spoken: <strong>じゃありません</strong>).</li></ol><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Category</th><th>Tense</th><th>Affirmative (polite)</th><th>Negative (polite)</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Type I (e.g. 高い)</td><td>Non-past</td><td>高いです</td><td>高くありません</td></tr><tr><td>Type II (e.g. 静か)</td><td>Non-past</td><td>静かです</td><td>静かではありません（じゃありません）</td></tr></tbody></table><p><strong>では</strong> can be replaced by <strong>じゃ</strong> in spoken Japanese.</p><ul><li>東京は寒いです。 — Tokyo is cold.</li><li>あの映画は面白くありません。 — That movie is not interesting.</li><li>先生は親切です。 — The teacher is kind.</li><li>日本語は簡単ではありません。 — Japanese is not easy.</li></ul><p><strong>いい:</strong> Conjugate from the older form <strong>よい</strong>. Negative of いいです → <strong>よくありません</strong>.</p><ul><li>成績はよくありません。 — The grades are not good.</li></ul><h2 id="n1-は-n2-が-〜" tabindex="-1">N₁ は N₂ が 〜 <a class="header-anchor" href="#n1-は-n2-が-〜" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;N₁ は N₂ が 〜&quot;">​</a></h2><p><strong>Rule:</strong> <strong>は</strong> marks the <strong>topic</strong> (what the sentence is about); <strong>が</strong> marks the <strong>subject</strong> of the predicate that follows. In <strong>A は B が X です</strong>, A is usually a person or a whole thing, B is a part or aspect of A, and <strong>B が X です</strong> describes a property of A. This “topic + part/aspect” pattern is common in Japanese and often differs from how Chinese or English packages the same idea.</p><ul><li>象は鼻が長いです。 — Elephants have long trunks. / An elephant’s trunk is long.</li><li>富士山は形がいいです。 — Mt. Fuji has a nice shape.</li><li>今日は天気がいいです。 — The weather is good today.</li><li>彼女は性格が明るいです。 — She has a cheerful personality.</li></ul><h2 id="あまり-そんなに-adjective-negative" tabindex="-1">あまり / そんなに + adjective (negative) <a class="header-anchor" href="#あまり-そんなに-adjective-negative" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;あまり / そんなに + adjective (negative)&quot;">​</a></h2><p><strong>Rule:</strong> The degree adverbs <strong>あまり</strong> and <strong>そんなに</strong> with a <strong>negative</strong> adjective mean “not very …” / “not that …”. Use them with the polite negative forms of Type I and Type II adjectives (e.g. 寒くありません, 難しくありません).</p><ul><li>A: 東京は寒いですか。 — Is Tokyo cold?</li><li>B: いいえ、あまり寒くありません。 — No, it’s not very cold.</li><li>日本語はそんなに難しくありません。 — Japanese is not that difficult.</li><li>このパソコンはそんなに遅くありません。 — This computer is not that slow.</li></ul><p><a id="reason-kara"></a></p><h2 id="〜から-reason" tabindex="-1">〜から（reason） <a class="header-anchor" href="#〜から-reason" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;〜から（reason）&quot;">​</a></h2><p><strong>Rule:</strong> <strong>から</strong> at the end of a clause or sentence marks a <strong>reason / cause</strong> (“because …”). It can stand alone as an answer or follow a statement that gives the conclusion first.</p><ul><li>（中村さんは）とても真面目ですから。 — Because (Nakamura-san) is very serious / diligent.</li><li>アイスクリームがいいです。暑いですから。 — Ice cream would be good. Because it’s hot.</li><li>A: 寒いですね。 — It’s cold, isn’t it?</li><li>B: そうですね。今日は雪ですから。 — Yes. Because it’s snowing today.</li></ul><h2 id="n-はどうですか-asking-about-nature-opinion" tabindex="-1">N はどうですか（asking about nature / opinion） <a class="header-anchor" href="#n-はどうですか-asking-about-nature-opinion" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;N はどうですか（asking about nature / opinion）&quot;">​</a></h2><p><strong>Rule:</strong> <strong>どう</strong> means “how / what is it like.” <strong>N はどうですか</strong> asks about the <strong>nature</strong> of something or the listener’s <strong>opinion</strong> of it. Answer with an adjectival predicate (or a short evaluative phrase).</p><ul><li>A: 日本の留学生生活はどうですか。 — How is life as an international student in Japan?</li><li>B: 順調ですよ。 — It’s going well.</li><li>A: 日本語はどうですか。 — How is Japanese?</li><li>B: 難しいです。 — It’s difficult.</li><li>A: この映画はどうですか。 — How is this movie?</li><li>B: とても面白いです。 — It’s very interesting.</li></ul><h2 id="〜よ-assertion-emphasis" tabindex="-1">〜よ（assertion / emphasis） <a class="header-anchor" href="#〜よ-assertion-emphasis" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;〜よ（assertion / emphasis）&quot;">​</a></h2><p><strong>Rule:</strong> Sentence-final <strong>よ</strong> marks the speaker’s <strong>assertion</strong>, strong claim, or emphatic tone — often new information or a firm reminder for the listener.</p><ul><li>中村さんのおかげですよ。 — It’s thanks to Nakamura-san.</li><li>山羊座の人はみんな真面目ですよ。 — Capricorns are all serious / diligent.</li><li>日本語はそんなに難しくありませんよ。 — Japanese isn’t that difficult.</li></ul><h2 id="〜ね-soft-exclamation" tabindex="-1">〜ね（soft exclamation） <a class="header-anchor" href="#〜ね-soft-exclamation" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;〜ね（soft exclamation）&quot;">​</a></h2><p><strong>Rule:</strong> Sentence-final <strong>ね</strong> can mark a <strong>light exclamation / shared impression</strong>. Read it with a <strong>falling</strong> tone (↘). For a softer stretch, lengthen it to <strong>ねえ</strong>.</p><ul><li>由加ちゃんはすごいですね。↘ — Yuka-chan is really amazing.</li><li>この洋服、かわいいですね。↘ — These clothes are cute.</li><li>由加ちゃんのこのスマホケース、かわいいですね。↘ — Yuka-chan, this phone case of yours is cute.</li></ul><h2 id="adjective-predicates-polite-past" tabindex="-1">Adjective predicates (polite past) <a class="header-anchor" href="#adjective-predicates-polite-past" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Adjective predicates (polite past)&quot;">​</a></h2><p><strong>Rule:</strong> Past forms of adjectival predicates differ by type.</p><ol><li><strong>Past affirmative:</strong> Type I → drop <strong>い</strong>, then <strong>かったです</strong>; Type II → <strong>A₂ + でした</strong>.</li><li><strong>Past negative:</strong> Type I → drop <strong>い</strong>, then <strong>く + ありませんでした</strong>; Type II → <strong>A₂ + ではありませんでした</strong> (spoken: <strong>じゃありませんでした</strong>).</li></ol><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Category</th><th>Tense</th><th>Affirmative (polite)</th><th>Negative (polite)</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Type I (e.g. 高い)</td><td>Non-past</td><td>高いです</td><td>高くありません</td></tr><tr><td></td><td>Past</td><td>高かったです</td><td>高くありませんでした</td></tr><tr><td>Type II (e.g. 静か)</td><td>Non-past</td><td>静かです</td><td>静かではありません（じゃありません）</td></tr><tr><td></td><td>Past</td><td>静かでした</td><td>静かではありませんでした（じゃありませんでした）</td></tr></tbody></table><p><strong>では</strong> can be replaced by <strong>じゃ</strong> in spoken Japanese.</p><ul><li>A: お客さんは多かったですか。 — Were there many customers?</li><li>B: ううん、平日の昼間ですから、ばらばらでしたよ。 — No — it was a weekday afternoon, so they were sparse.</li><li>子供の時、生活は豊かではありませんでした。 — When I was a child, life was not affluent.</li><li>10年前の家賃はそんなに高くありませんでした。 — Ten years ago, the rent wasn’t that high.</li></ul><h2 id="ない-negation-adjective–noun-quadrants" tabindex="-1">ない（negation） / adjective–noun quadrants <a class="header-anchor" href="#ない-negation-adjective–noun-quadrants" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;ない（negation） / adjective–noun quadrants&quot;">​</a></h2><p><strong>Rule:</strong> <strong>ない</strong> means “not / does not exist.” It is itself a <strong>Type I</strong> adjective, and it also works as an <strong>auxiliary adjective</strong> after other adjectives (and in noun/Type II patterns) to mark negation.</p><p>Adding <strong>です</strong> after <strong>ない</strong> is more polite and pairs with the <strong>ありません</strong> forms:</p><ul><li>Non-past: <strong>ありません</strong> ＝ <strong>ないです</strong></li><li>Past: <strong>ありませんでした</strong> ＝ <strong>なかったです</strong></li></ul><p>Together, tense (non-past / past) × polarity (affirmative / negative) make the <strong>adjective / noun quadrants</strong> (四象限).</p><h3 id="adjective-quadrant-polite" tabindex="-1">Adjective quadrant (polite) <a class="header-anchor" href="#adjective-quadrant-polite" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Adjective quadrant (polite)&quot;">​</a></h3><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Category</th><th>Tense</th><th>Affirmative</th><th>Negative</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Type I (e.g. 高い)</td><td>Non-past</td><td>高いです</td><td>高くありません ／ 高くないです</td></tr><tr><td></td><td>Past</td><td>高かったです</td><td>高くありませんでした ／ 高くなかったです</td></tr><tr><td>Type II (e.g. 静か)</td><td>Non-past</td><td>静かです</td><td>静かではありません（じゃありません） ／ 静かではないです（じゃないです）</td></tr><tr><td></td><td>Past</td><td>静かでした</td><td>静かではありませんでした（じゃありませんでした） ／ 静かではなかったです（じゃなかったです）</td></tr></tbody></table><h3 id="noun-quadrant-polite" tabindex="-1">Noun quadrant (polite) <a class="header-anchor" href="#noun-quadrant-polite" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Noun quadrant (polite)&quot;">​</a></h3><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Class</th><th>Tense</th><th>Affirmative</th><th>Negative</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Noun (e.g. 学生)</td><td>Non-past</td><td>学生です</td><td>学生ではありません（じゃありません） ／ 学生ではないです（じゃないです）</td></tr><tr><td></td><td>Past</td><td>学生でした</td><td>学生ではありませんでした（じゃありませんでした） ／ 学生ではなかったです（じゃなかったです）</td></tr></tbody></table><p><strong>では</strong> can be replaced by <strong>じゃ</strong> in spoken Japanese.</p><h2 id="connecting-adjectives" tabindex="-1">Connecting adjectives <a class="header-anchor" href="#connecting-adjectives" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Connecting adjectives&quot;">​</a></h2><p><strong>Rule:</strong> To link adjectives in a parallel / sequential chain, use the <strong>continuative</strong> form of the <strong>preceding</strong> adjective. The form depends only on that adjective, not on what follows. Tense is carried by the <strong>final</strong> predicate; the middle form does not mark tense.</p><ul><li><p><strong>Type I:</strong> drop <strong>い</strong> → <strong>くて</strong> (A₁ い → く + て)</p></li><li><p><strong>Type II:</strong> <strong>A₂ + で</strong></p></li><li><p>映画の主人公は背が高くて、魅力的です。 — The movie’s protagonist is tall and charming. <em>(高くて = Type I; 魅力的 = Type II)</em></p></li><li><p>昨日の試験問題は複雑で、難しかったです。 — Yesterday’s exam questions were complicated and difficult. <em>(複雑で = Type II; final past on 難しい)</em></p></li><li><p>映画の主人公は背が高くて、かっこいいです。 — …tall and cool. <em>(Type I + Type I — still 高くて)</em></p></li><li><p>映画の主人公は背が高くて、魅力的です。 — …tall and charming. <em>(Type I + Type II — still 高くて)</em></p></li><li><p>昨日の映画の主人公は背が高くて、魅力的でした。 — Yesterday’s protagonist was tall and charming. <em>(middle stays 高くて; past only on でした)</em></p></li></ul><h2 id="n-まで-endpoint" tabindex="-1">N まで（endpoint） <a class="header-anchor" href="#n-まで-endpoint" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;N まで（endpoint）&quot;">​</a></h2><p><strong>Rule:</strong> <strong>まで</strong> follows a noun and marks an <strong>endpoint / limit</strong> (“until / up to / as far as”). It works with <strong>time</strong> nouns (this lesson) and with <strong>place</strong> nouns for a spatial endpoint. <strong>N まで</strong> can also take <strong>です</strong> as a noun-predicate sentence.</p><ul><li>わたしは昨日、遅くまで日本語の試験でした。 — Yesterday my Japanese exam went until late.</li><li>今日の授業はここまでです。 — Today’s class ends here. / That’s as far as today’s lesson goes.</li><li>あの歌手のライブツアーは来月20日までです。 — That singer’s live tour runs until the 20th of next month.</li></ul><h2 id="全然-adjective-negative" tabindex="-1">全然 + adjective (negative) <a class="header-anchor" href="#全然-adjective-negative" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;全然 + adjective (negative)&quot;">​</a></h2><p><strong>Rule:</strong> Like <strong>あまり</strong>, <strong>全然</strong> is a degree adverb that goes with a <strong>negative</strong> adjective: “not … at all” / “completely not …”. In spoken Japanese, <strong>全然</strong> alone can answer “not at all.”</p><ul><li>A: 昨日の試験は難しかったですか。 — Was yesterday’s exam difficult?</li><li>B: 全然難しくなかったですよ。 — It wasn’t difficult at all.</li><li>わたしは運動が全然好きではありません。 — I don’t like exercise at all.</li><li>彼女の料理は全然美味しくありません。 — Her cooking isn’t delicious at all.</li><li>A: 日本語の試験は難しかったですか。 — Was the Japanese exam difficult?</li><li>B: ううん、全然。 — No, not at all.</li></ul><h2 id="n-とか-listing-examples" tabindex="-1">N とか（listing examples） <a class="header-anchor" href="#n-とか-listing-examples" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;N とか（listing examples）&quot;">​</a></h2><p><strong>Rule:</strong> <strong>とか</strong> attaches to nouns to <strong>list examples</strong> (things like …). Common in spoken Japanese; it can appear once or several times. With <strong>とか</strong>, topic/subject/object particles <strong>は / が / を</strong> can be omitted.</p><ul><li>A: どんな飲み物が好きですか。 — What kind of drinks do you like?</li><li>B: ミルクティーとか、コーラとか。 — Milk tea, cola, that sort of thing.</li><li>木曜日の午後以降とかはどうですか。 — How about something like Thursday afternoon onward?</li></ul><h2 id="adjectives-modifying-nouns-attributive-form-连体形" tabindex="-1">Adjectives modifying nouns (attributive form / 连体形) <a class="header-anchor" href="#adjectives-modifying-nouns-attributive-form-连体形" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Adjectives modifying nouns (attributive form / 连体形)&quot;">​</a></h2><p><strong>Rule:</strong> Besides acting as predicates, adjectives can sit <strong>before a noun</strong> and modify it. That form is the <strong>attributive form</strong> (连体形).</p><ul><li><p><strong>Type I</strong> (い-adjectives): <strong>A₁ + noun</strong> — the adjective attaches to the noun <strong>directly</strong> (keeps its <strong>い</strong> ending).</p></li><li><p><strong>Type II</strong> (な-adjectives): <strong>A₂ + な + noun</strong> — insert <strong>な</strong> before the noun. Some books call these <strong>な-adjectives</strong> for that reason.</p></li><li><p>白いパソコン — white computer <em>(Type I: 白い + パソコン)</em></p></li><li><p>きれいな町 — clean / beautiful town <em>(Type II: きれい + な + 町)</em></p></li></ul><h2 id="n-が-subject-—-は-vs-が-as-subject-markers" tabindex="-1">N が（subject）— は vs が as subject markers <a class="header-anchor" href="#n-が-subject-—-は-vs-が-as-subject-markers" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;N が（subject）— は vs が as subject markers&quot;">​</a></h2><div class="important custom-block github-alert"><p class="custom-block-title">IMPORTANT</p><p></p><p>Easy to confuse for a long time: both <strong>は</strong> and <strong>が</strong> can mark what looks like a “subject,” but they answer different information questions. <strong>が</strong> = the subject is <strong>new / unknown</strong> to the listener (focus on the noun before <strong>が</strong>). <strong>は</strong> = the subject is <strong>already known</strong> (old information); focus falls on what <strong>comes after</strong> <strong>は</strong>.</p></div><p><strong>Rule:</strong> When introducing or identifying something the listener does not yet have as given, use <strong>が</strong>. Once that referent is established in the conversation, switch to <strong>は</strong>. Wh-questions about the subject (<strong>だれが</strong>, <strong>何が</strong>, …) take <strong>が</strong>; the answer usually keeps <strong>が</strong> on the identified item, then later sentences about that person/thing use <strong>は</strong>.</p><ul><li>ほら、あそこの男の人。あの人<strong>は</strong>中村さんのお兄さんですよ。 — Look, that man over there. He is Nakamura’s older brother. <em>(あの人 is already pointed out → <strong>は</strong>; focus on who he is.)</em></li><li>A: へえ、小学校の時の記念写真ですか。中村さんは、これ？ — Oh, an elementary-school commemorative photo? Nakamura, is this you?</li><li>B: 違いますよ。これ<strong>が</strong>わたしです。 — No. <strong>This</strong> is me. <em>(identifying which one → <strong>が</strong>; focus on これ.)</em></li><li>こちらのパソコン<strong>が</strong>、今お得ですよ。 — <strong>This</strong> PC is a good deal right now. <em>(picking out this one as new focus → <strong>が</strong>.)</em></li><li>A: 日本語<strong>は</strong>難しいですか。 — Is Japanese difficult? <em>(topic = Japanese → <strong>は</strong>.)</em></li><li>B: ええ、文法<strong>が</strong>難しいです。 — Yes — <strong>the grammar</strong> is what’s difficult. <em>(specifying the hard part as new focus → <strong>が</strong>.)</em></li><li>A: だれ<strong>が</strong>留学生ですか。 — <strong>Who</strong> is the international student? <em>(だれが — always <strong>が</strong>.)</em></li><li>B: あの人<strong>が</strong>留学生で、ジョージさんです。ジョージさん<strong>は</strong>今、先生の研究室にいます。 — <strong>That person</strong> is the international student — George. George is in the teacher’s lab now. <em>(identify with <strong>が</strong>, then known George → <strong>は</strong>.)</em></li></ul><h2 id="n-と同じ-same-as-n" tabindex="-1">N と同じ（same as N） <a class="header-anchor" href="#n-と同じ-same-as-n" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;N と同じ（same as N）&quot;">​</a></h2><p><strong>Rule:</strong> <strong>同じ</strong> is a <strong>Type II</strong> adjective meaning “the same / identical.” Attach <strong>と</strong> after the noun you compare with: <strong>N と同じ</strong> = “the same as N.”</p><p><strong>Exception (easy miss):</strong> When <strong>同じ</strong> modifies a noun <strong>attributively</strong>, do <strong>not</strong> add <strong>な</strong> — write <strong>同じ + noun</strong> directly (not <em>同じな</em>).</p><ul><li>白いパソコンと同じ大きさです。 — It’s the same size as the white computer.</li><li>李さんは中村さんと同じ大学です。 — Li is at the same university as Nakamura.</li><li>経済学部は法学部と同じ建物です。 — The economics department is in the same building as the law department.</li></ul><h2 id="n-がほしいです-want-n" tabindex="-1">N がほしいです（want N） <a class="header-anchor" href="#n-がほしいです-want-n" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;N がほしいです（want N）&quot;">​</a></h2><p><strong>Rule:</strong> <strong>ほしい</strong> is a <strong>Type I</strong> adjective meaning “want (to obtain something).” Conjugate it like other Type I adjectives. The thing wanted is usually marked with <strong>が</strong>: <strong>N がほしいです</strong> ≈ “I want N.”</p><p><strong>Person restriction:</strong> Use <strong>ほしい</strong> mainly for the <strong>speaker’s</strong> desire (first person). Avoid it for third-person wants; for the listener, it is mostly OK with close family/friends.</p><p><strong>ほしい</strong> can also modify a noun attributively (<strong>ほしい + noun</strong>).</p><ul><li>（私は）新しいスーツケースがほしいです。 — I want a new suitcase.</li><li>地下鉄がなかった時は、車がほしかったです。 — When there was no subway, I wanted a car.</li><li>A: ほしいものはありますか。 — Is there anything you want?</li><li>B: ありますよ。お金がほしいです。 — Yes. I want money.</li></ul><h2 id="clause1-が、clause2-adversative-softener" tabindex="-1">Clause₁ が、Clause₂（adversative / softener） <a class="header-anchor" href="#clause1-が、clause2-adversative-softener" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Clause₁ が、Clause₂（adversative / softener）&quot;">​</a></h2><p><strong>Rule:</strong> Mid-sentence <strong>が</strong> can link two clauses with a <strong>contrast / turning</strong> sense (“…, but …” / “although Clause₁, Clause₂”). Soften the tone after set phrases like <strong>すみませんが</strong> / <strong>失礼ですが</strong> — the adversative feel is weak and often need not be translated as “but.” Clause₂ can also be <strong>omitted</strong>, leaving a trailing <strong>が</strong> that softens a request or wish.</p><ul><li>歴史の授業は難しいですが、面白いです。 — History class is difficult, but interesting.</li><li>失礼ですが、お名前は？ — Excuse me — what’s your name?</li><li>新しいパソコンがほしいのですが。 — I’d like a new computer… <em>(Clause₂ left open / soft request.)</em></li></ul><h2 id="n1-も-n2-も-both-and" tabindex="-1">N₁ も N₂ も（both … and …） <a class="header-anchor" href="#n1-も-n2-も-both-and" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;N₁ も N₂ も（both … and …）&quot;">​</a></h2><p><strong>Rule:</strong> Put <strong>も</strong> after each of two nouns in parallel: <strong>N₁ も N₂ も ～</strong>. It lists both items as sharing the same predicate — “both N₁ and N₂ …” / “N₁ and N₂ alike …”.</p><ul><li>兄も姉も会社員です。 — Both my older brother and older sister are company employees.</li><li>日本料理も中華料理も美味しいです。 — Both Japanese food and Chinese food are delicious.</li><li>メモリーもストレージも大きいです。 — Both the memory and the storage are large.</li></ul><h2 id="comparison-patterns-比較" tabindex="-1">Comparison patterns（比較） <a class="header-anchor" href="#comparison-patterns-比較" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Comparison patterns（比較）&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Japanese has several fixed frames for comparing two nouns. The four below are the core set from this lesson.</p><h3 id="_1-n1-は-n2-より" tabindex="-1">(1) N₁ は N₂ より ～ <a class="header-anchor" href="#_1-n1-は-n2-より" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;(1) N₁ は N₂ より ～&quot;">​</a></h3><p><strong>Rule:</strong> <strong>より</strong> marks the <strong>baseline</strong> of comparison. <strong>N₁ は N₂ より ～</strong> means N₁ has more of the predicate property than N₂ (“N₁ is …-er than N₂”). When the gap is large, add <strong>ずっと</strong> (“much more …”).</p><ul><li>中国は日本より広いです。 — China is larger than Japan.</li><li>（こちらのパソコンは）さっきのよりメモリーがずっと大きいですよ。 — (This PC’s) memory is much larger than the one just now.</li><li>スマホはパソコンより値段が高いです。 — Smartphones cost more than computers.</li></ul><h3 id="_2-n2-より-n1-のほうが" tabindex="-1">(2) N₂ より N₁ のほうが ～ <a class="header-anchor" href="#_2-n2-より-n1-のほうが" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;(2) N₂ より N₁ のほうが ～&quot;">​</a></h3><p><strong>Rule:</strong> Same comparison, different packaging: <strong>N₂ より N₁ のほうが ～</strong> = “compared with N₂, N₁ is the one that is …”. <strong>ほう</strong> (方) means “side / one of the two.” Often used to <strong>answer</strong> pattern (4); in answers, <strong>N₂ より</strong> is frequently dropped → <strong>N₁ のほうが ～</strong>.</p><ul><li>日本より中国のほうが広いです。 — Compared with Japan, China is larger.</li><li>パソコンよりスマホのほうが値段が高いです。 — Compared with PCs, smartphones are more expensive.</li></ul><h3 id="_3-n2-は-n1-ほど-negative" tabindex="-1">(3) N₂ は N₁ ほど ～（negative） <a class="header-anchor" href="#_3-n2-は-n1-ほど-negative" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;(3) N₂ は N₁ ほど ～（negative）&quot;">​</a></h3><p><strong>Rule:</strong> <strong>ほど</strong> after a noun marks <strong>degree</strong>. With a <strong>negative</strong> predicate, <strong>N₂ は N₁ ほど ～ない</strong> means N₂ does <strong>not</strong> reach N₁’s degree — “N₂ is not as … as N₁.”</p><ul><li>日本は中国ほど広くありません。 — Japan is not as large as China.</li><li>パソコンはスマホほど値段が高くありません。 — Computers are not as expensive as smartphones.</li></ul><h3 id="_4-n1-と-n2-とどちら-のほう-が-か" tabindex="-1">(4) N₁ と N₂ とどちら（のほう）が ～か <a class="header-anchor" href="#_4-n1-と-n2-とどちら-のほう-が-か" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;(4) N₁ と N₂ とどちら（のほう）が ～か&quot;">​</a></h3><p><strong>Rule:</strong> Ask which of two is more …: <strong>N₁ と N₂ とどちら（のほう）が ～か</strong>. Answer with pattern (2), usually just <strong>N のほうが ～</strong>.</p><ul><li>A: 中国と日本とどちら（のほう）が広いですか。 — Between China and Japan, which is larger?</li><li>B: 中国のほうが広いです。 — China is larger.</li><li>A: パソコンとスマホとどちらが高いですか。 — Between a PC and a smartphone, which is more expensive?</li><li>B: スマホのほうが高いです。 — The smartphone is.</li></ul><p><a id="reason-node-noun"></a></p><h2 id="n-なので、-cause-and-reason-ii" tabindex="-1">N なので、～（cause and reason II） <a class="header-anchor" href="#n-なので、-cause-and-reason-ii" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;N なので、～（cause and reason II）&quot;">​</a></h2><p><strong>Rule:</strong> <strong>ので</strong> connects two clauses to express a cause and its result. With a noun predicate, use <strong>N + な + ので</strong>, meaning “because (it is) N, …” (see Lesson 5, Grammar 1). Compared with <strong>から</strong> (Lesson 9, Grammar 4), <strong>ので</strong> emphasizes an objective cause and sounds softer and more formal.</p><ul><li>僕は神奈川県の湘南の出身なので、僕も『スラムダンク』が大好きですよ。 — Because I’m from Shōnan in Kanagawa Prefecture, I love <em>Slam Dunk</em>, too.</li><li>雨なので、運動会は中止です。 — Because of the rain, the sports day is canceled.</li><li>学生なので、お金がないです。 — Because I’m a student, I don’t have any money.</li></ul><h2 id="n-が-object-marker-が-iii" tabindex="-1">N が～（object marker が III） <a class="header-anchor" href="#n-が-object-marker-が-iii" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;N が～（object marker が III）&quot;">​</a></h2><p><strong>Rule:</strong> Besides marking the subject, the particle <strong>が</strong> can also mark an object. In this lesson, <strong>が</strong> marks the object of an ability or skill level. These expressions are mainly <strong>Type II (na-) adjectives</strong> and <strong>verbs</strong>.</p><ul><li><p><strong>Type II (na-) adjectives:</strong> N が 上手／下手 (good／poor at), 好き／嫌い (like／dislike), 得意／苦手 (strong／weak at) です。</p></li><li><p><strong>Verbs:</strong> N が できます (can do), わかります (understand).</p></li><li><p>私は運動が嫌いです。 — I don’t like exercise.</p></li><li><p>辛いものが苦手です。 — I can’t handle spicy food.</p></li><li><p>兄は歴史が得意です。 — My older brother is good at history.</p></li><li><p>先生の話がよくわかりません。 — I don’t understand the teacher’s explanation very well.</p></li><li><p>父は料理ができます。 — My father can cook.</p></li></ul><h2 id="n1の中で-n2がいちばん-superlative-で-marks-range" tabindex="-1">（N₁の中で）N₂がいちばん～（superlative; で marks range） <a class="header-anchor" href="#n1の中で-n2がいちばん-superlative-で-marks-range" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;（N₁の中で）N₂がいちばん～（superlative; で marks range）&quot;">​</a></h2><p><strong>Rule:</strong> Building on the comparison patterns, this is the <strong>superlative</strong>: within the range given by <strong>N₁</strong>, <strong>N₂</strong> has the most of the quality the predicate describes. The particle <strong>で</strong> after a noun limits the range, and the adverb <strong>いちばん</strong> means &quot;most&quot;. A superlative singles out the most outstanding person or thing among three or more. Translate as &quot;(Among/In N₁,) N₂ is the most ～.&quot;</p><ul><li>日本の漫画の中で、『スラムダンク』がいちばん好きです。 — Among Japanese manga, I like <em>Slam Dunk</em> the most.</li><li>スポーツの中でバスケットボールがいちばん好きです。 — Among sports, I like basketball the most.</li><li>クラスの中で、李さんがいちばん頭がいいです。 — In the class, Li is the smartest.</li><li>日本料理の中で、お寿司がいちばん好きです。 — Among Japanese dishes, I like sushi the most.</li></ul><h2 id="は-が、-は-。-contrastive-は-ii" tabindex="-1">～は～が、～は～。（contrastive は II） <a class="header-anchor" href="#は-が、-は-。-contrastive-は-ii" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;～は～が、～は～。（contrastive は II）&quot;">​</a></h2><p><strong>Rule:</strong> In an adversative sentence joined by <strong>が</strong> (&quot;but&quot;), when the two clauses have a similar shape and contrast with each other, both topics are marked with <strong>は</strong>. The <strong>は … は</strong> pair carries the contrast.</p><ul><li>バスケットボールはできませんが、サッカーとテニスはすこしできます。 — I can&#39;t play basketball, but I can play a little soccer and tennis.</li><li>日本語は上手ですが、英語は下手です。 — I&#39;m good at Japanese, but bad at English.</li><li>昨日は寒かったですが、今日はとても暑いです。 — It was cold yesterday, but it&#39;s very hot today.</li><li>私は学生ですが、妹は会社員です。 — I&#39;m a student, but my younger sister is a company employee.</li></ul><h2 id="n-に-target-of-a-state-or-quality" tabindex="-1">N に～（target of a state or quality） <a class="header-anchor" href="#n-に-target-of-a-state-or-quality" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;N に～（target of a state or quality）&quot;">​</a></h2><p><strong>Rule:</strong> After a noun, the particle <strong>に</strong> marks the target that a following ability, attitude, or quality applies to, like English &quot;for / about / at N&quot;. The predicate is usually an adjective of <strong>quality</strong>, <strong>state</strong>, or <strong>ability</strong>, such as 必要, 便利, or 詳しい.</p><ul><li>マスクは外出に必要です。 — Masks are necessary for going out.</li><li>横の中国語訳は日本語学習に便利です。 — The Chinese translation beside it is useful for studying Japanese.</li><li>兄は中国の歴史にも、日本の歴史にも詳しいです。 — My older brother knows a lot about both Chinese and Japanese history.</li><li>駅の近くのアパートは、買い物に便利です。 — The apartment near the station is convenient for shopping.</li></ul><h2 id="の-ん-です-explanation-i-adjectives-and-nouns" tabindex="-1">～の（／ん）です（explanation I: adjectives and nouns） <a class="header-anchor" href="#の-ん-です-explanation-i-adjectives-and-nouns" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;～の（／ん）です（explanation I: adjectives and nouns）&quot;">​</a></h2><div class="important custom-block github-alert"><p class="custom-block-title">IMPORTANT</p><p></p><p>The most important pattern in this lesson. <strong>～の（／ん）です</strong> turns a plain statement into an <strong>explanation</strong>: it presents the fact as the reason, background, or &quot;the thing is…&quot; behind the situation. Speech usually shortens it to <strong>～んです</strong>. Chinese and English have no direct equivalent, so it is usually left untranslated.</p></div><p><strong>Connection:</strong></p><ul><li>N + な + の（／ん）です</li><li>Aɪ (い-adjective) + の（／ん）です</li><li>Aɪɪ (な-adjective) + な + の（／ん）です</li></ul><p><strong>Rule:</strong> This pattern is very common in conversation. It attaches to the <strong>attributive (連体形) form of an adjective</strong> or to <strong>N + な</strong>, and in speech <strong>～んです</strong> is preferred. It states the speaker&#39;s own view, or explains or clarifies a situation. It has three main uses.</p><h3 id="_1-explaining-a-situation-or-giving-a-reason" tabindex="-1">(1) Explaining a situation or giving a reason <a class="header-anchor" href="#_1-explaining-a-situation-or-giving-a-reason" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;(1) Explaining a situation or giving a reason&quot;">​</a></h3><p>Gives the <strong>reason or cause</strong> behind something already mentioned, mostly to explain the speaker&#39;s own circumstances.</p><ul><li>実は『キャプテン翼』も『テニスの王子様』も好きなんですよ。 — Actually, I like both <em>Captain Tsubasa</em> and <em>The Prince of Tennis</em>, too.</li><li>すみません、今はちょっと忙しいんです。 — Sorry, I&#39;m a bit busy right now.</li><li>私は冷たい飲み物が苦手なんです。 — I don&#39;t really like cold drinks.</li></ul><h3 id="_2-agreement-and-realization" tabindex="-1">(2) Agreement and realization <a class="header-anchor" href="#_2-agreement-and-realization" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;(2) Agreement and realization&quot;">​</a></h3><p>What was said before becomes the reason for the のです sentence. The speaker uses it to <strong>accept or confirm</strong> the conclusion, as in &quot;so that&#39;s how it is&quot;, often confirming their own guess.</p><ul><li>由加さんは本当に漫画ファンなのですね。 — So you really are a manga fan, Yuka.</li></ul><h3 id="_3-giving-background-for-a-tactful-suggestion-or-request" tabindex="-1">(3) Giving background for a tactful suggestion or request <a class="header-anchor" href="#_3-giving-background-for-a-tactful-suggestion-or-request" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;(3) Giving background for a tactful suggestion or request&quot;">​</a></h3><p><strong>～の（／ん）ですが、～</strong> sets out the background first, and what the speaker actually wants is linked to it. Often the sentence stops at <strong>が</strong> and the listener infers the request.</p><ul><li>新しいパソコンがほしいのですが。 — I&#39;d like a new computer… (to a shop assistant)</li><li>マックの最新モデルがいちばん軽いのですが。 — The latest Mac is the lightest one… (would you consider it?)</li><li>あのう、テレビの音が大きいのですが。 — Um, the TV is rather loud… (could you turn it down?)</li></ul><p><strong>Notes:</strong> The first example is how you tell a shop assistant what you need. The second explains a fact while hinting &quot;why not consider it?&quot;. The third politely describes the situation and implies the request to lower the volume.</p><h2 id="n-place-へ-に-movement-verb-direction-of-movement" tabindex="-1">N（place）＋へ（／に）＋movement verb（direction of movement） <a class="header-anchor" href="#n-place-へ-に-movement-verb-direction-of-movement" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;N（place）＋へ（／に）＋movement verb（direction of movement）&quot;">​</a></h2><p><strong>Rule:</strong> After a noun for a place, the particle <strong>へ</strong> marks the <strong>direction</strong> of movement. It pairs with movement verbs such as <strong>行く</strong> (go), <strong>来る</strong> (come), and <strong>帰る</strong> (go/come home). In this use <strong>へ</strong> is pronounced <strong>e</strong>, not <em>he</em>. <strong>へ</strong> can also be replaced by <strong>に</strong>.</p><ul><li>毎朝、会社へ行きます。 — I go to work every morning.</li><li>いつ帰りますか。 — When are you going home?</li><li>去年、家族と大阪に来ました。 — I came to Osaka with my family last year.</li></ul><h2 id="疑問詞-も-total-negation" tabindex="-1">疑問詞 ＋ も（total negation） <a class="header-anchor" href="#疑問詞-も-total-negation" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;疑問詞 ＋ も（total negation）&quot;">​</a></h2><p><strong>Rule:</strong> A <strong>question word + も</strong> used with a <strong>negative predicate</strong> gives total negation: &quot;no one / nothing / none / nowhere&quot;. 誰も means no one, 何も nothing, どれも none of them, and どこにも nowhere. A particle like <strong>に</strong> goes between the question word and <strong>も</strong>, as in どこ<strong>に</strong>も.</p><ul><li>夜中の博物館には誰もいません。 — There is no one in the museum in the middle of the night.</li><li>引き出しには何もありません。 — There is nothing in the drawer.</li><li>ここのカレー料理はどれもおいしくないです。 — None of the curry dishes here taste good.</li><li>この週末はどこにも行きません。 — I&#39;m not going anywhere this weekend.</li></ul><h2 id="疑問詞-か-indefinite-some" tabindex="-1">疑問詞 ＋ か（indefinite: some-） <a class="header-anchor" href="#疑問詞-か-indefinite-some" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;疑問詞 ＋ か（indefinite: some-）&quot;">​</a></h2><p><strong>Rule:</strong> A <strong>question word + か</strong> makes it <strong>indefinite</strong>: someone, something, some number of. 誰か means someone, 何人か several people, いくつか a few, and 何回か a few times. It works in <strong>statements</strong> as well as questions. When <strong>疑問詞か</strong> appears in a question, the question becomes an ordinary <strong>yes/no question</strong>, so the answer usually starts with <strong>はい</strong> or <strong>いいえ</strong>. Compare 疑問詞 + も + negative, which means &quot;no one / nothing&quot;.</p><ul><li>あのレストランにお客さんが何人かいます。 — There are a few customers in that restaurant.</li><li>特に重要な理由がいくつかあります。 — There are a few especially important reasons.</li><li>A：あのう、すみません。誰かいますか。 — A: Um, excuse me. Is anyone there?</li><li>B：はい。 — B: Yes.</li><li>以前、何回か来ました。 — I&#39;ve been here a few times before.</li></ul><h2 id="n-から-starting-point" tabindex="-1">N から（starting point） <a class="header-anchor" href="#n-から-starting-point" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;N から（starting point）&quot;">​</a></h2><p><strong>Rule:</strong> After a noun of <strong>time</strong>, <strong>place</strong>, or <strong>order</strong>, the particle <strong>から</strong> marks the <strong>starting point</strong>, like English &quot;from&quot;. <strong>から</strong> can be followed directly by <strong>です</strong> to make a noun-predicate sentence (&quot;…starts from N&quot;). The starting-point <strong>から</strong> also pairs with <strong>まで</strong>, which marks the end point: <strong>N₁ から N₂ まで</strong>, &quot;from N₁ to N₂&quot;.</p><ul><li>体のトレーニングは明日からです。 — Physical training starts tomorrow.</li><li>今から成績の発表です。 — We&#39;ll now announce the results.</li><li>ここからはテストの内容です。 — From here on is what&#39;s on the test.</li><li>会議は今月の4日から6日までです。 — The meeting runs from the 4th to the 6th of this month.</li></ul><h2 id="n-じゃありませんか-surprised-isn-t-it" tabindex="-1">N じゃありませんか（surprised &quot;isn&#39;t it…?&quot;） <a class="header-anchor" href="#n-じゃありませんか-surprised-isn-t-it" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;N じゃありませんか（surprised &quot;isn&#39;t it…?&quot;）&quot;">​</a></h2><p><strong>Rule:</strong> This is the <strong>rhetorical-question</strong> form of the noun sentence <strong>N です</strong>, like &quot;isn&#39;t it N?&quot; or &quot;why, it&#39;s N!&quot;. It doesn&#39;t really ask anything. It carries <strong>surprise or exclamation</strong>, and is used mainly in <strong>speech</strong>.</p><ul><li>とても有名な研究所じゃありませんか。 — Why, this is a really famous research institute!</li><li>もう12時50分じゃありませんか。 — It&#39;s already 12:50!</li><li>あ、あの人は、鈴木さんじゃありませんか。 — Oh, isn&#39;t that Suzuki over there?</li></ul><h2 id="けど。-sentence-final-softened-contrast" tabindex="-1">…けど。（sentence-final softened contrast） <a class="header-anchor" href="#けど。-sentence-final-softened-contrast" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;…けど。（sentence-final softened contrast）&quot;">​</a></h2><p><strong>Rule:</strong> The conjunction <strong>けど</strong> is short for <strong>けれども</strong> and marks contrast (&quot;but&quot;). Here it comes at the <strong>end of the sentence</strong> as a <strong>softened contrast</strong>: the speaker stops partway and leaves the second half unsaid. That makes the tone <strong>gentle and indirect</strong>, and it is used mostly in speech. Besides <strong>けど</strong>, けれども also shortens to <strong>けれど</strong> and <strong>けども</strong>.</p><ul><li>これから会議なんですけど。 — We&#39;re about to start the meeting… (so, if you don&#39;t mind)</li><li>そろそろお時間ですけれども。 — It&#39;s about time…</li><li>A：中村さんはいますか。 — A: Is Nakamura here?</li><li>B：はい、私ですけど。 — B: Yes, that&#39;s me… (what is it?)</li></ul><hr><div class="note custom-block github-alert"><p class="custom-block-title">NOTE</p><p></p><p><strong>Start of verbs（動詞）.</strong> Everything above covers nouns, adjectives and particles. From here on, the notes cover verbs.</p></div><h2 id="verb-conjugation-classes-動詞の活用分類" tabindex="-1">Verb conjugation classes（動詞の活用分類） <a class="header-anchor" href="#verb-conjugation-classes-動詞の活用分類" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Verb conjugation classes（動詞の活用分類）&quot;">​</a></h2><p><strong>Rule:</strong> Sorting verbs by <strong>conjugation class</strong> is the first step to using verb sentences well. Traditional Japanese school grammar and today&#39;s popular textbooks classify verbs a little differently, but mostly only the <strong>names</strong> differ. The <strong>dictionary form</strong>, also called the <strong>basic form</strong>, is the unconjugated form a verb has as a dictionary entry.</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Class</th><th>Japanese name</th><th>Form</th><th>Examples</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Class I</td><td>五段動詞</td><td>Dictionary form ends in a <strong>う-row kana</strong></td><td>休む、願う、わかる、ある</td></tr><tr><td>Class II</td><td>一段動詞</td><td>Dictionary form ends in <strong>る</strong>, preceded by an <strong>い-row or え-row kana</strong></td><td>寝る、起きる、忘れる</td></tr><tr><td>Class III</td><td>サ変動詞</td><td><strong>する</strong> on its own, or <strong>Sino-Japanese word + する</strong></td><td>する、勉強する、運動する、運転する</td></tr><tr><td>Class III</td><td>カ変動詞</td><td>Only one verb: <strong>来る</strong></td><td>来る</td></tr></tbody></table><h2 id="v-ます形-polite-form-and-the-ます-stem" tabindex="-1">V ます形（polite form and the ます-stem） <a class="header-anchor" href="#v-ます形-polite-form-and-the-ます-stem" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;V ます形（polite form and the ます-stem）&quot;">​</a></h2><p><strong>Rule:</strong> In use, a verb usually takes the polite ending <strong>ます</strong> after its dictionary form. Before <strong>ます</strong> is added, the verb changes according to its <strong>conjugation class</strong> (see the table above). This change is called <strong>verb conjugation（動詞の活用）</strong>.</p><h3 id="class-i-五段動詞" tabindex="-1">Class I（五段動詞） <a class="header-anchor" href="#class-i-五段動詞" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Class I（五段動詞）&quot;">​</a></h3><p>Change the final kana from the <strong>う-row</strong> to the matching <strong>い-row</strong> kana, then add <strong>ます</strong>.</p><ul><li>休む: む → み + ます = 休みます</li><li>願う: う → い + ます = 願います</li><li>ある: る → り + ます = あります</li></ul><h3 id="class-ii-一段動詞" tabindex="-1">Class II（一段動詞） <a class="header-anchor" href="#class-ii-一段動詞" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Class II（一段動詞）&quot;">​</a></h3><p>Drop the final <strong>る</strong>, then add <strong>ます</strong>.</p><ul><li>起きる: drop る + ます = 起きます</li><li>寝る: drop る + ます = 寝ます</li><li>忘れる: drop る + ます = 忘れます</li></ul><h3 id="class-iii-サ変・カ変" tabindex="-1">Class III（サ変・カ変） <a class="header-anchor" href="#class-iii-サ変・カ変" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Class III（サ変・カ変）&quot;">​</a></h3><ul><li><strong>サ変 する:</strong> する → し + ます = します; 勉強する → 勉強し + ます = 勉強します</li><li><strong>カ変 来る:</strong> 来る（くる）→ 来（き）+ ます = 来ます（きます）</li></ul><h3 id="the-ます-stem" tabindex="-1">The ます-stem <a class="header-anchor" href="#the-ます-stem" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;The ます-stem&quot;">​</a></h3><p>What is left after removing <strong>ます</strong> from the polite form is the <strong>ます-stem</strong>, labelled <strong>V ます形</strong> in these notes. Japanese school grammar calls it the <strong>（第一）連用形</strong>. For example, ある has the polite form <strong>あります</strong>, so its ます-stem is <strong>あり</strong>.</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Class</th><th>Dictionary form</th><th>Polite form</th><th>ます-stem（連用形）</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Class I</td><td>わかる</td><td>わかります</td><td>わかり</td></tr><tr><td>Class II</td><td>忘れる</td><td>忘れます</td><td>忘れ</td></tr><tr><td>Class III</td><td>する</td><td>します</td><td>し</td></tr><tr><td>Class III</td><td>来る（くる）</td><td>来ます（きます）</td><td>来（き）</td></tr></tbody></table><h2 id="n-time-に-point-in-time" tabindex="-1">N（time）に（point in time） <a class="header-anchor" href="#n-time-に-point-in-time" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;N（time）に（point in time）&quot;">​</a></h2><p><strong>Rule:</strong> After a time noun, the particle <strong>に</strong> marks the <strong>point in time</strong> when an action happens, like English &quot;at / on&quot;.</p><ul><li>日曜日の午後1時半に会いましょうか。 — Shall we meet at 1:30 on Sunday afternoon?</li><li>いつも夜9時に寝ます。 — I always go to bed at 9 p.m.</li><li>13日に中村さんの誕生日パーティーがあります。 — There&#39;s a birthday party for Nakamura on the 13th.</li></ul><h2 id="n-time-ごろ-approximate-time" tabindex="-1">N（time）＋ごろ（approximate time） <a class="header-anchor" href="#n-time-ごろ-approximate-time" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;N（time）＋ごろ（approximate time）&quot;">​</a></h2><p><strong>Rule:</strong> The suffix <strong>ごろ</strong> (also written <strong>頃</strong>) after a specific time noun means <strong>&quot;around / about&quot;</strong> that time. With <strong>ごろ</strong>, the time-point particle <strong>に</strong> can be <strong>dropped</strong>: both 2時ごろ帰ります and 2時ごろに帰ります work.</p><ul><li>いつも夜11時ごろ寝ます。 — I always go to bed around 11 p.m.</li><li>今日は2時ごろに帰ります。 — Today I&#39;ll go home around 2.</li><li>3月ごろ、桜が咲きます。 — Cherry blossoms bloom around March.</li><li>2000年ごろから、車が多くなりました。 — From around 2000, there have been more and more cars.</li></ul><h2 id="数量詞-くらい-ぐらい-about-approximately" tabindex="-1">数量詞＋くらい／ぐらい（about, approximately） <a class="header-anchor" href="#数量詞-くらい-ぐらい-about-approximately" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;数量詞＋くらい／ぐらい（about, approximately）&quot;">​</a></h2><p><strong>Rule:</strong> <strong>くらい</strong> (also <strong>ぐらい</strong>) after a quantity word for time, money, or a count means <strong>&quot;about / approximately&quot;</strong> that amount, like &quot;or so&quot; in English.</p><div class="tip custom-block github-alert"><p class="custom-block-title">TIP</p><p>Compare it with <strong>ごろ</strong>: ごろ marks an approximate <strong>point in time</strong> (2時ごろ, around 2 o&#39;clock), while くらい marks an approximate <strong>amount or length</strong> (2時間ぐらい, about two hours).</p></div><ul><li>発表は全部で2時間ぐらいでした。 — The presentation took about two hours in all.</li><li>食費は毎月3万円ぐらいです。 — Food costs about 30,000 yen a month.</li><li>教室には学生が5人ぐらいいます。 — There are about five students in the classroom.</li></ul><h2 id="なる-change-of-state" tabindex="-1">なる（change of state） <a class="header-anchor" href="#なる-change-of-state" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;なる（change of state）&quot;">​</a></h2><p><strong>Rule:</strong> <strong>なる</strong> (&quot;to become&quot;) describes a thing or its state <strong>changing naturally</strong>. It combines with nouns, adjectives, and verbs; this lesson covers nouns and adjectives.</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Word type</th><th>Pattern</th><th>Example</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Noun</td><td><strong>N ＋ に ＋ なる</strong></td><td>会社員 → 会社員になる</td></tr><tr><td>い-adjective</td><td><strong>drop い → く ＋ なる</strong></td><td>寒い → 寒くなる</td></tr><tr><td>な-adjective</td><td><strong>A ＋ に ＋ なる</strong></td><td>静か → 静かになる</td></tr></tbody></table><ul><li>11月の初めから寒くなります。 — It starts getting cold from early November.</li><li>体調が大分よくなりました。 — My health has gotten a lot better.</li><li>日本語がかなり上手になりました。 — My Japanese has gotten quite good.</li><li>今週末のイベントは中止になりました。 — This weekend&#39;s event has been called off.</li><li>でも、午後から眠くなるから、何もできなくなります。 — But from the afternoon on I get sleepy, so I can&#39;t do anything anymore.</li></ul><p><strong>Note:</strong> 何もできなくなります means &quot;(it comes to the point that) I can&#39;t do anything.&quot; できない is the negative of <strong>できる</strong> (can, be able to). When a negative ない form is followed by なる, <strong>ない becomes なく</strong>: できない → <strong>できなくなる</strong>, a change into not being able to.</p><h2 id="vましょう-vましょうか-invitation-let-s-shall-we" tabindex="-1">Vましょう／Vましょうか（invitation: let&#39;s / shall we） <a class="header-anchor" href="#vましょう-vましょうか-invitation-let-s-shall-we" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Vましょう／Vましょうか（invitation: let&#39;s / shall we）&quot;">​</a></h2><p><strong>Rule:</strong> <strong>ます-stem ＋ ましょう</strong> invites the listener to do something <strong>together</strong> with you, often with <strong>いっしょに</strong>: &quot;let&#39;s …&quot;. Adding the question particle <strong>か</strong> (<strong>Vましょうか</strong>) makes it <strong>softer</strong>: you are asking whether the other person agrees.</p><ul><li>そろそろ帰りましょう。 — Let&#39;s head home soon.</li><li>A：昼休みは、あそこの店へ行きましょうか。 — A: Shall we go to that place over there at lunch?</li><li>B：そうしましょう。 — B: Yes, let&#39;s do that.</li><li>A：今晩、家でゲームしましょうか。 — A: Shall we play games at my place tonight?</li><li>B：いいですね。 — B: Sounds good.</li></ul><h2 id="ほうがいい-advice-it-s-better-that-with-nouns-and-adjectives" tabindex="-1">～ほうがいい（advice: it&#39;s better that…, with nouns and adjectives） <a class="header-anchor" href="#ほうがいい-advice-it-s-better-that-with-nouns-and-adjectives" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;～ほうがいい（advice: it&#39;s better that…, with nouns and adjectives）&quot;">​</a></h2><p><strong>Rule:</strong> <strong>ほう</strong> means &quot;side / direction&quot;. Attaching <strong>ほうがいい</strong> gives the speaker&#39;s <strong>advice</strong> to the listener: &quot;… would be better.&quot;</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Word type</th><th>Pattern</th><th>Example</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Noun</td><td><strong>N ＋ の ＋ ほうがいい</strong></td><td>効率的なもの → 効率的なもののほうがいい</td></tr><tr><td>い-adjective</td><td><strong>dictionary form ＋ ほうがいい</strong></td><td>近い → 近いほうがいい</td></tr><tr><td>な-adjective</td><td><strong>A ＋ な ＋ ほうがいい</strong></td><td>安全 → 安全なほうがいい</td></tr></tbody></table><ul><li>もっと効率的なもののほうがいいですよ。 — Something more efficient would be better.</li><li>新しいアパートは会社に近いほうがいいです。 — It&#39;s better if the new apartment is close to the office.</li><li>毎日の食材は安全なほうがいいです。 — Everyday ingredients had better be safe.</li></ul><h2 id="transitive-verbs-and-を-marking-the-direct-object" tabindex="-1">Transitive verbs and を（marking the direct object） <a class="header-anchor" href="#transitive-verbs-and-を-marking-the-direct-object" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Transitive verbs and を（marking the direct object）&quot;">​</a></h2><p><strong>Rule:</strong> Japanese verbs can be <strong>intransitive</strong> or <strong>transitive</strong>. An intransitive verb does not take a direct object; a transitive verb can. Some verbs can be used in both ways. With a transitive verb, put <strong>を</strong> after the noun that is its direct object.</p><ul><li>本を読みます。 — I read a book.</li><li>資料を調べます。 — I examine the materials.</li></ul><p>When the direct object becomes the <strong>topic</strong> or is marked as <strong>also</strong>, <strong>は</strong> or <strong>も</strong> can replace <strong>を</strong>.</p><ul><li>メールはよく送りますが、手紙はあまり書きません。 — I often send emails, but I do not write letters very often.</li><li>休みの日は、よく映画を見ます。買い物もします。 — On my days off, I often watch movies. I also go shopping.</li></ul><h2 id="n-で-place-of-an-action-means-or-tool" tabindex="-1">N で（place of an action; means or tool） <a class="header-anchor" href="#n-で-place-of-an-action-means-or-tool" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;N で（place of an action; means or tool）&quot;">​</a></h2><h3 id="place-of-an-action" tabindex="-1">Place of an action <a class="header-anchor" href="#place-of-an-action" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Place of an action&quot;">​</a></h3><p><strong>Rule:</strong> Put <strong>で</strong> after a place noun to show <strong>where an action takes place</strong>. In English, it often corresponds to &quot;at&quot; or &quot;in&quot;.</p><ul><li>伊藤先生のゼミでデータを集めました。 — I collected data in Professor Ito&#39;s seminar.</li><li>私はよく喫茶店で小説を読みます。 — I often read novels at a coffee shop.</li><li>日本では、口頭試験はどんな準備をしますか。 — In Japan, how do you prepare for oral exams?</li></ul><h3 id="means-or-tool" tabindex="-1">Means or tool <a class="header-anchor" href="#means-or-tool" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Means or tool&quot;">​</a></h3><p><strong>Rule:</strong> Put <strong>で</strong> after a noun to show <strong>the tool or means used to do an action</strong>. In English, it often corresponds to &quot;with&quot; or &quot;by&quot;.</p><ul><li>スマホで外国語を勉強します。 — I study foreign languages with my smartphone.</li><li>インターネットで資料を調べます。 — I look up information on the internet.</li><li>日本人はお箸で料理を食べます。 — Japanese people eat food with chopsticks.</li></ul><h2 id="n-に-recipient-or-target-of-an-action" tabindex="-1">N に（recipient or target of an action） <a class="header-anchor" href="#n-に-recipient-or-target-of-an-action" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;N に（recipient or target of an action）&quot;">​</a></h2><p><strong>Rule:</strong> Put <strong>に</strong> after a noun to mark the <strong>person an action is directed toward</strong>. Depending on the verb, this person may be the recipient of something, someone you meet, or someone you ask.</p><ul><li>中村さんにメールで資料を送ります。 — I send the materials to Nakamura by email.</li><li>午後、ジョージさんに会います。 — I will meet George in the afternoon.</li><li>友達に簡単なやり方を聞きます。 — I ask a friend for a simple way to do it.</li></ul><h2 id="n-なら-bringing-up-a-topic" tabindex="-1">N なら（bringing up a topic） <a class="header-anchor" href="#n-なら-bringing-up-a-topic" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;N なら（bringing up a topic）&quot;">​</a></h2><p><strong>Rule:</strong> Put <strong>なら</strong> after a noun to bring that noun forward as the <strong>topic</strong> of what follows. The topic is often something already mentioned or something the listener can infer from the conversation. <strong>N なら、～</strong> can mean &quot;as for N, …&quot; or, when responding to someone&#39;s interest or request, &quot;if it&#39;s N you&#39;re talking about, …&quot;.</p><ul><li>日本語の小説なら、家には何冊かありますよ。 — As for Japanese novels, I have a few at home.</li><li>A：新しいパソコンがほしいんですが。 — I&#39;d like a new computer.</li><li>B：パソコンなら、こちらがいいですよ。 — If you&#39;re looking for a computer, this one is good.</li><li>A：一緒に喫茶店に行きましょうか。 — Shall we go to a coffee shop together?</li><li>B：コーヒーなら、スタバのがいいですね。 — If we&#39;re going for coffee, Starbucks would be good.</li></ul><h2 id="n-について-about-a-topic" tabindex="-1">N について（about a topic） <a class="header-anchor" href="#n-について-about-a-topic" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;N について（about a topic）&quot;">​</a></h2><p><strong>Rule:</strong> Put <strong>について</strong> after a noun to introduce the subject being discussed: <strong>&quot;about N&quot;</strong>. It can be followed by <strong>です</strong> to form a predicate, or it can appear before a verb phrase describing what someone says, writes, or asks about that topic.</p><ul><li>経済のグローバリゼーションについてです。 — It&#39;s about economic globalization.</li><li>先週の旅行について作文を書きました。 — I wrote an essay about last week&#39;s trip.</li><li>今日の授業について、何か質問はありますか。 — Do you have any questions about today&#39;s class?</li></ul><h2 id="v-ます-stem-activity-noun-に-movement-verb-purpose-of-movement" tabindex="-1">V ます-stem／activity noun ＋ に ＋ movement verb（purpose of movement） <a class="header-anchor" href="#v-ます-stem-activity-noun-に-movement-verb-purpose-of-movement" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;V ます-stem／activity noun ＋ に ＋ movement verb（purpose of movement）&quot;">​</a></h2><p><strong>Rule:</strong> Put <strong>に</strong> after a verb&#39;s <strong>ます-stem</strong> or a noun that names an activity, then use a movement verb such as <strong>行く</strong>, <strong>来る</strong>, or <strong>帰る</strong>. This shows the <strong>purpose</strong> of the movement: &quot;go/come (somewhere) to do …&quot;. With a <strong>する</strong> verb used as an activity noun, use its noun part without <strong>する</strong> before <strong>に</strong>.</p><ul><li>教室に忘れ物を取りに行きます。 — I will go to the classroom to pick up something I left behind.</li><li>サークルの仲間たちとお花見に行きます。 — I will go cherry blossom viewing with members of my club.</li><li>昨日中村さんのお家に遊びに行きました。 — Yesterday I went to Nakamura&#39;s house to hang out.</li><li>散歩に出かけましょうか。 — Shall we go out for a walk?</li><li>資料を届けに来ました。 — I came to deliver the materials.</li></ul><h2 id="v-ます-stem-ながら-doing-two-things-at-once" tabindex="-1">V ます-stem ＋ ながら（doing two things at once） <a class="header-anchor" href="#v-ます-stem-ながら-doing-two-things-at-once" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;V ます-stem ＋ ながら（doing two things at once）&quot;">​</a></h2><p><strong>Rule:</strong> Put <strong>ながら</strong> after a verb&#39;s <strong>ます-stem</strong> to show that two actions happen <strong>at the same time</strong> or continue in alternation. In English, it often means &quot;while doing …&quot;.</p><ul><li>考えながら、パワーポイントを作ります。 — I make a PowerPoint presentation while thinking it through.</li><li>弟はいつも音楽を聴きながら、宿題をします。 — My younger brother always does his homework while listening to music.</li><li>お茶をしながら、話しましょうか。 — Shall we talk over tea?</li></ul><h2 id="quantity-expression-も-a-subjectively-large-amount" tabindex="-1">Quantity expression ＋ も（a subjectively large amount） <a class="header-anchor" href="#quantity-expression-も-a-subjectively-large-amount" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Quantity expression ＋ も（a subjectively large amount）&quot;">​</a></h2><p><strong>Rule:</strong> Put <strong>も</strong> after a quantity expression to show that the speaker considers the amount <strong>large or more than expected</strong>. The number itself does not have to be objectively large: <strong>３人も</strong> can mean &quot;as many as three people&quot; when three feels like a lot in context.</p><ul><li>教室に学生が３人もいます。 — There are as many as three students in the classroom.</li><li>議論は３日も続きました。 — The discussion went on for three whole days.</li><li>日系企業を５社も訪問しました。 — I visited as many as five Japanese-affiliated companies.</li></ul><p><strong>何度も:</strong> <strong>何度</strong> (&quot;how many times&quot;) is a question expression, but <strong>何度も</strong> means <strong>&quot;many times&quot;</strong>. The exact number is unspecified; the speaker still presents it as a lot.</p><ul><li>あの映画は何度も見ましたよ。 — I&#39;ve seen that movie many times.</li></ul><h2 id="でしょう-seeking-confirmation-i" tabindex="-1">～でしょう（seeking confirmation I） <a class="header-anchor" href="#でしょう-seeking-confirmation-i" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;～でしょう（seeking confirmation I）&quot;">​</a></h2><p><strong>Rule:</strong> Add <strong>でしょう</strong> after a noun or adjective predicate and say it with <strong>rising intonation</strong> to seek confirmation or make a rhetorical point: &quot;… right?&quot; or &quot;… isn&#39;t it?&quot;. In casual speech, <strong>でしょう</strong> is often shortened to <strong>でしょ</strong>.</p><ul><li>李さんも留学生でしょ？ — Li is also an international student, right?</li><li>勉強のほうが大事でしょう？ — Studying is more important, isn&#39;t it?</li><li>タバコを止めましょうよ。体に悪いでしょう？ — Let&#39;s quit smoking. It&#39;s bad for your health, isn&#39;t it?</li></ul><h2 id="n-のための-n-purpose-for" tabindex="-1">N のための N（purpose: for） <a class="header-anchor" href="#n-のための-n-purpose-for" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;N のための N（purpose: for）&quot;">​</a></h2><p><strong>Rule:</strong> <strong>ため</strong> expresses a purpose, often translated as <strong>&quot;for&quot;</strong>. When a noun names the purpose, put <strong>の</strong> after it: <strong>N のため</strong>. If another noun follows, add a second <strong>の</strong> to link them: <strong>N₁ のための N₂</strong>, meaning &quot;N₂ for N₁&quot;.</p><ul><li>ここは小学生のための教室です。 — This is a classroom for elementary school students.</li><li>海外旅行のためのタオルを買いました。 — I bought a towel for an overseas trip.</li><li>期末試験のための勉強計画を考えます。 — I&#39;ll make a study plan for the final exams.</li></ul><h2 id="の-ん-です-explanation-ii-verbs" tabindex="-1">～の（／ん）です（explanation II: verbs） <a class="header-anchor" href="#の-ん-です-explanation-ii-verbs" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;～の（／ん）です（explanation II: verbs）&quot;">​</a></h2><p><strong>Connection:</strong> Verb plain form ＋ <strong>の（／ん）です</strong>. In speech, <strong>んです</strong> is more common.</p><p><strong>Rule:</strong> This is the verb form of the explanatory <strong>～の（／ん）です</strong> pattern introduced above for nouns and adjectives. In a question, it asks the listener to <strong>explain</strong> something, often in response to what was just said or to a situation the speaker has noticed or inferred.</p><ul><li>A：一緒に本屋へ行きませんか。 — Shall we go to the bookstore together?</li><li>B：本屋へ何をしに行くんですか。 — What are we going to the bookstore to do?</li><li>A：もう帰るんですか。 — Are you going home already?</li><li>B：ええ、もう時間なので。 — Yes, it&#39;s already time to leave.</li><li>A：お腹が痛いんですか。 — Does your stomach hurt?</li><li>B：ええ、ちょっと。 — Yes, a little.</li></ul><h2 id="v-ませんか-gentle-invitation" tabindex="-1">V ませんか（gentle invitation） <a class="header-anchor" href="#v-ませんか-gentle-invitation" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;V ませんか（gentle invitation）&quot;">​</a></h2><p><strong>Rule:</strong> Use the polite negative question <strong>V ませんか</strong> to invite someone to do something <strong>together</strong>. It sounds gentler than <strong>V ましょうか</strong> and can be used with <strong>いっしょに</strong> (&quot;together&quot;). In English, it often means &quot;Would you like to …?&quot; or &quot;Shall we …?&quot;.</p><ul><li>来週のサークル活動にいっしょに参加しませんか。 — Would you like to join me for next week&#39;s club activity?</li><li>明日、映画を見に行きませんか。 — Would you like to go see a movie tomorrow?</li><li>ここで写真を撮りませんか。 — Shall we take a photo here?</li></ul><h2 id="plain-verb-forms-動詞の簡体形式" tabindex="-1">Plain verb forms（動詞の簡体形式） <a class="header-anchor" href="#plain-verb-forms-動詞の簡体形式" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Plain verb forms（動詞の簡体形式）&quot;">​</a></h2><p><strong>Rule:</strong> Like noun and adjective predicates, verb predicates have both <strong>polite（敬体）</strong> and <strong>plain（簡体）</strong> forms. The chart below compares their four combinations of tense and polarity. <strong>Vる</strong> stands for the dictionary form of a verb; not every dictionary-form verb actually ends in <strong>る</strong>. The plain negative and past forms are covered in more detail in later grammar sections.</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Tense</th><th>Polarity</th><th>Polite form</th><th>Plain form</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Non-past (present/future)</td><td>Affirmative</td><td>V ます</td><td>V る（dictionary form）</td></tr><tr><td>Non-past (present/future)</td><td>Negative</td><td>V ません</td><td>V ない</td></tr><tr><td>Past</td><td>Affirmative</td><td>V ました</td><td>V た</td></tr><tr><td>Past</td><td>Negative</td><td>V ませんでした</td><td>V なかった</td></tr></tbody></table><p><strong>Usage:</strong> Polite forms are common in formal situations and courteous conversation. Plain forms sound more familiar and are common with friends and family. In writing, plain style gives statements a direct tone and is common in essays and news reports.</p><h2 id="v-る-dictionary-form" tabindex="-1">V る（dictionary form） <a class="header-anchor" href="#v-る-dictionary-form" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;V る（dictionary form）&quot;">​</a></h2><p><strong>Rule:</strong> The <strong>dictionary form</strong> is a verb&#39;s basic form, the one used for its entry in a dictionary. It is also the <strong>plain non-past affirmative</strong> form. It is sometimes called the <strong>base form</strong> or <strong>original form</strong>. The pairs below show how it compares with the polite <strong>ます</strong> form.</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Style</th><th>Example</th><th>English</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Polite</td><td>スマホでウェブサイトを閲覧します。</td><td>I browse websites on my smartphone.</td></tr><tr><td>Plain</td><td>スマホでウェブサイトを閲覧する。</td><td>I browse websites on my smartphone.</td></tr><tr><td>Polite</td><td>よく徹夜します。</td><td>I often stay up all night.</td></tr><tr><td>Plain</td><td>よく徹夜する。</td><td>I often stay up all night.</td></tr></tbody></table><h2 id="v-ない-plain-negative-form" tabindex="-1">V ない（plain negative form） <a class="header-anchor" href="#v-ない-plain-negative-form" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;V ない（plain negative form）&quot;">​</a></h2><p><strong>Rule:</strong> Add the negative auxiliary adjective <strong>ない</strong> to a verb&#39;s negative stem to make its <strong>plain non-past negative</strong> form. How the stem is made depends on the verb class.</p><h3 id="class-i-五段動詞-1" tabindex="-1">Class I（五段動詞） <a class="header-anchor" href="#class-i-五段動詞-1" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Class I（五段動詞）&quot;">​</a></h3><p>Change the final kana from the <strong>う-row</strong> to the corresponding <strong>あ-row</strong> kana, then add <strong>ない</strong>. For a verb ending in <strong>う</strong>, change <strong>う</strong> to <strong>わ</strong> before <strong>ない</strong>.</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Dictionary form</th><th>Change</th><th>Plain negative</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>使う</td><td>う → わ</td><td>使わない</td></tr><tr><td>歩く</td><td>く → か</td><td>歩かない</td></tr><tr><td>帰る</td><td>る → ら</td><td>帰らない</td></tr></tbody></table><h3 id="class-ii-一段動詞-1" tabindex="-1">Class II（一段動詞） <a class="header-anchor" href="#class-ii-一段動詞-1" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Class II（一段動詞）&quot;">​</a></h3><p>Drop the final <strong>る</strong>, then add <strong>ない</strong>.</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Dictionary form</th><th>Plain negative</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>見る</td><td>見ない</td></tr><tr><td>食べる</td><td>食べない</td></tr><tr><td>集める</td><td>集めない</td></tr></tbody></table><h3 id="class-iii-サ変・カ変-1" tabindex="-1">Class III（サ変・カ変） <a class="header-anchor" href="#class-iii-サ変・カ変-1" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Class III（サ変・カ変）&quot;">​</a></h3><ul><li><strong>する:</strong> する → しない; 利用する → 利用しない.</li><li><strong>来る（くる）:</strong> 来る → 来ない（こない）.</li></ul><p><strong>Exception:</strong> The plain negative of <strong>ある</strong> is <strong>ない</strong>, not <em>あらない</em>.</p><ul><li>この週末は家に帰らない。 — I&#39;m not going home this weekend.</li><li>今の学生はあまり運動しない。 — Students these days do not exercise much.</li></ul><h3 id="plain-past-negative-v-なかった" tabindex="-1">Plain past negative: V なかった <a class="header-anchor" href="#plain-past-negative-v-なかった" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Plain past negative: V なかった&quot;">​</a></h3><p>To make the plain past negative, change the final <strong>い</strong> of <strong>V ない</strong> to <strong>かった</strong>: <strong>V ない → V なかった</strong>. This corresponds to the polite <strong>V ませんでした</strong>.</p><ul><li>宿題が多くて、昨夜、あまり寝なかった。 — I had a lot of homework, so I did not sleep much last night.</li><li>A：昨日のビールは？ — What about yesterday&#39;s beer?</li><li>B：冷蔵庫だよ。 — It&#39;s in the fridge.</li><li>A：ビールは冷蔵庫になかったよ。 — There wasn&#39;t any beer in the fridge.</li></ul><h2 id="verb-adnominal-form-動詞連体形" tabindex="-1">Verb adnominal form（動詞連体形） <a class="header-anchor" href="#verb-adnominal-form-動詞連体形" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Verb adnominal form（動詞連体形）&quot;">​</a></h2><div class="important custom-block github-alert"><p class="custom-block-title">IMPORTANT</p><p></p><p><strong>Rule:</strong> A verb can go <strong>directly before a noun</strong> to describe or identify that noun. This is called the verb&#39;s <strong>adnominal form</strong>. Its forms are the same as the verb&#39;s plain forms; choose the appropriate tense and polarity for the meaning. For example, <strong>行く場所</strong> means &quot;the place (someone) will go,&quot; while <strong>飛ばない可能性</strong> means &quot;the possibility that (something) will not fly.&quot;</p></div><ul><li>授業の予習や復習、課題をやる時間。 — Time to prepare for class, review, and do assignments.</li><li>明日行く場所はどこですか。 — Where are you going tomorrow?</li><li>雨なので、飛行機が飛ばない可能性があります。 — Because it&#39;s raining, there&#39;s a chance the plane won&#39;t fly.</li></ul><h2 id="n1-period-に-n2-quantity-v-frequency" tabindex="-1">N₁（period）に N₂（quantity）V（frequency） <a class="header-anchor" href="#n1-period-に-n2-quantity-v-frequency" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;N₁（period）に N₂（quantity）V（frequency）&quot;">​</a></h2><p><strong>Rule:</strong> Put <strong>に</strong> after a noun naming a <strong>period</strong> (often a span of time), then give a <strong>number of times</strong> to say how often an action occurs within that period. It corresponds to English &quot;… times per …&quot; or &quot;once every …&quot;.</p><ul><li>週に３回運動する。 — I exercise three times a week.</li><li>２か月に１回ぐらい映画を観る。 — I watch a movie about once every two months.</li><li>１年に１回中国へ帰る。 — I go back to China once a year.</li></ul><h2 id="n1-についての-n2-n2-about-n1" tabindex="-1">N₁ についての N₂（N₂ about N₁） <a class="header-anchor" href="#n1-についての-n2-n2-about-n1" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;N₁ についての N₂（N₂ about N₁）&quot;">​</a></h2><p><strong>Rule:</strong> <strong>N について</strong> means &quot;about N&quot; and introduces a topic. Add <strong>の</strong> when that phrase modifies a following noun: <strong>N₁ についての N₂</strong>, meaning &quot;N₂ about N₁&quot;.</p><ul><li>こちらは大学生のライフスタイルについてのアンケートです。 — This is a survey about university students&#39; lifestyles.</li><li>このウェブサイトには、来週の口頭試験についての情報はない。 — This website has no information about next week&#39;s oral exam.</li><li>来週は期末試験なので、明日から経済学についての本を読む。 — Since final exams are next week, I&#39;ll start reading a book about economics tomorrow.</li></ul><h2 id="ほとんど-あまり-negative-verb-hardly-not-much" tabindex="-1">ほとんど／あまり ＋ negative verb（hardly / not much） <a class="header-anchor" href="#ほとんど-あまり-negative-verb-hardly-not-much" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;ほとんど／あまり ＋ negative verb（hardly / not much）&quot;">​</a></h2><p><strong>Rule:</strong> Use the adverb <strong>ほとんど</strong> with a negative verb to mean <strong>&quot;hardly ever&quot;</strong> or <strong>&quot;almost not at all&quot;</strong>. <strong>あまり</strong> also pairs with a negative verb and means <strong>&quot;not very often&quot;</strong> or <strong>&quot;not much&quot;</strong>. <strong>ほとんど</strong> generally expresses a stronger degree of negation than <strong>あまり</strong>.</p><ul><li>インターネットやSNSはほとんど利用しません。 — I hardly ever use the internet or social media.</li><li>漫画はときどき読みますが、アニメはほとんど見ません。 — I sometimes read manga, but I hardly ever watch anime.</li><li>Eメールはあまり使いません。 — I don&#39;t use email much.</li><li>梅雨の季節はあまり晴れません。 — It isn&#39;t sunny very often during the rainy season.</li></ul><h2 id="n-particle-の-n-modifying-a-noun" tabindex="-1">N ＋ particle ＋ の ＋ N（modifying a noun） <a class="header-anchor" href="#n-particle-の-n-modifying-a-noun" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;N ＋ particle ＋ の ＋ N（modifying a noun）&quot;">​</a></h2><p><strong>Rule:</strong> Besides linking two nouns directly, as in <strong>私の車</strong> (&quot;my car&quot;), <strong>の</strong> can follow a meaningful particle phrase such as <strong>N と</strong>, <strong>N から</strong>, <strong>N で</strong>, or <strong>N へ</strong>. The resulting phrase modifies the noun that follows.</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Action phrase</th><th>Noun phrase</th><th>English</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>友達と食事する</td><td>友達との食事</td><td>a meal with a friend</td></tr><tr><td>明日からゴールデンウィークが始まる</td><td>明日からのゴールデンウィーク</td><td>Golden Week starting tomorrow</td></tr><tr><td>デパートで買い物する</td><td>デパートでの買い物</td><td>shopping at a department store</td></tr><tr><td>大学の授業などに出席する</td><td>大学の授業などへの出席</td><td>attendance at university classes and similar activities</td></tr></tbody></table><p><strong>Note:</strong> There is no <strong>N にの N</strong> form. When the phrase means <strong>toward / to</strong> a destination or direction, use <strong>N への N</strong> instead.</p><ul><li>雨の影響で、外国からの観光客が減少しました。 — Because of the rain, there were fewer tourists from abroad.</li><li>後輩との食事会はとても楽しかったです。 — The meal with my junior classmates was a lot of fun.</li><li>台風の影響で、香港へのフライトはキャンセルになりました。 — Because of the typhoon, the flight to Hong Kong was canceled.</li></ul><h2 id="n1-や-n2-など-listing-examples" tabindex="-1">N₁ や N₂ など（listing examples） <a class="header-anchor" href="#n1-や-n2-など-listing-examples" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;N₁ や N₂ など（listing examples）&quot;">​</a></h2><p><strong>Rule:</strong> Use <strong>や</strong> to list two or more <strong>examples</strong> rather than every item in a group. <strong>など</strong> means &quot;and so on&quot; or &quot;among other things&quot;. The two can appear together as <strong>N₁ や N₂ など</strong>, or <strong>など</strong> can follow a noun on its own.</p><ul><li>インターネットやSNSなど — The internet, social media, and so on.</li><li>A：デパートに何がありますか。 — What is there in the department store?</li><li>B：映画館や本屋などがあります。 — There is a movie theater and a bookstore, among other things.</li><li>A：いつもどこで服を買いますか。 — Where do you usually buy clothes?</li><li>B：ユニクロやタオバオで買います。 — I buy them from places like Uniqlo and Taobao.</li></ul><h2 id="あげる-くれる-もらう-giving-and-receiving" tabindex="-1">あげる／くれる／もらう（giving and receiving） <a class="header-anchor" href="#あげる-くれる-もらう-giving-and-receiving" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;あげる／くれる／もらう（giving and receiving）&quot;">​</a></h2><p><strong>Rule:</strong> Japanese uses different verbs for giving and receiving depending on <strong>who gives, who receives, and whose viewpoint the speaker takes</strong>. These verbs describe the transfer of an item. Once the context makes the participants clear, Japanese often leaves the subject unstated.</p><h3 id="あげる-give-to-someone-else" tabindex="-1">あげる（give to someone else） <a class="header-anchor" href="#あげる-give-to-someone-else" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;あげる（give to someone else）&quot;">​</a></h3><p><strong>Pattern:</strong> <strong>N₁（giver／わたし）は N₂（recipient）に N（item）をあげる</strong>.</p><p><strong>あげる</strong> means &quot;give&quot; when the speaker gives something to another person. If both people are third parties, it also works when the speaker presents them neutrally or takes the giver&#39;s viewpoint.</p><ul><li>（わたしは）彼女に花をあげました。 — I gave her flowers.</li><li>日本では、バレンタインデーには女性が男性にチョコレートをあげます。 — In Japan, women give men chocolate on Valentine&#39;s Day.</li><li>妹が友達にお土産をあげました。 — My younger sister gave her friend a souvenir.</li></ul><p><strong>やる</strong> can also mean &quot;give&quot;. It is traditionally used for someone of lower status or for plants and animals, though it is less common in modern usage.</p><ul><li>花に水をやります。 — I water the flowers.</li></ul><h3 id="くれる-someone-gives-to-me-or-my-in-group" tabindex="-1">くれる（someone gives to me or my in-group） <a class="header-anchor" href="#くれる-someone-gives-to-me-or-my-in-group" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;くれる（someone gives to me or my in-group）&quot;">​</a></h3><p><strong>Pattern:</strong> <strong>N₁（giver）は N₂（recipient／わたし）に N（item）をくれる</strong>.</p><p><strong>くれる</strong> means &quot;give&quot; when someone else gives something to the speaker or to someone the speaker identifies with. The giver is someone other than the speaker. If both people are third parties, the speaker takes the recipient&#39;s viewpoint.</p><ul><li>お正月に祖父がお年玉を（わたしに）くれました。 — My grandfather gave me New Year&#39;s gift money.</li><li>社長が妹に花をくれました。 — The company president gave my younger sister flowers.</li></ul><h3 id="もらう-receive-from-someone" tabindex="-1">もらう（receive from someone） <a class="header-anchor" href="#もらう-receive-from-someone" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;もらう（receive from someone）&quot;">​</a></h3><p><strong>Pattern:</strong> <strong>N₁（recipient／わたし）は N₂（giver）に／から N（item）をもらう</strong>.</p><p><strong>もらう</strong> means &quot;receive&quot;. The giver is marked by <strong>に</strong> or <strong>から</strong>. The recipient is the speaker or someone the speaker identifies with; when both are third parties, the speaker takes the recipient&#39;s viewpoint.</p><ul><li>（わたしは）妹にプレゼントのアドバイスをもらいました。 — I got advice about a present from my younger sister.</li><li>佐藤：中村さんは李さんにどんなプレゼントをもらいましたか。 — Sato: What kind of present did you receive from Li, Nakamura?</li><li>中村：（わたしは）由加ちゃんからかわいいハンカチをもらいました。 — Nakamura: I received a cute handkerchief from Yuka.</li></ul><p><strong>いただく</strong> is the humble form of <strong>もらう</strong>. Use it when receiving something from someone you wish to show respect toward; it makes the expression more polite.</p><ul><li>みなさんから素敵なプレゼントをたくさんいただきました。 — I received many lovely gifts from everyone.</li></ul><h2 id="v-ます-stem-たい-want-to-do" tabindex="-1">V ます-stem ＋ たい（want to do） <a class="header-anchor" href="#v-ます-stem-たい-want-to-do" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;V ます-stem ＋ たい（want to do）&quot;">​</a></h2><p><strong>Rule:</strong> Add <strong>たい</strong> to a verb&#39;s <strong>ます-stem</strong> to express a desire to do that action: &quot;want to …&quot;. <strong>たい</strong> conjugates like an <strong>い-adjective</strong>, so it can become <strong>たくない</strong> (do not want to) or <strong>たかった</strong> (wanted to).</p><ul><li>あの会社で働きたいです。 — I want to work at that company.</li><li>今日はあまり出かけたくないです。 — I don&#39;t really want to go out today.</li><li>遊びに行きたかったのですが、台風で止めました。 — I wanted to go out for fun, but called it off because of the typhoon.</li></ul><p>With a transitive verb, the thing desired can be marked by <strong>が</strong> instead of <strong>を</strong>.</p><ul><li>おいしいものが食べたいです。 — I want to eat something delicious.</li></ul><p><strong>Person and politeness:</strong> <strong>V たいです</strong> normally states the <strong>speaker&#39;s own</strong> desire, often without an explicit subject. A direct <strong>V たいですか</strong> question about someone else&#39;s wishes can sound too personal or blunt, especially when speaking to a senior person or someone you do not know well. Ask about a preferred option more indirectly in that situation.</p><ul><li>○ 今日はあの人に会いたくありません。 — I don&#39;t want to see that person today.</li><li>△ 何が食べたいですか。 — What do you want to eat? <em>(Can sound too direct in a polite setting.)</em></li><li>○ お昼ご飯は何がいいんですか。 — What would you like for lunch?</li></ul><h2 id="か分かりません-i-don-t-know" tabindex="-1">～か分かりません（I don&#39;t know …） <a class="header-anchor" href="#か分かりません-i-don-t-know" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;～か分かりません（I don&#39;t know …）&quot;">​</a></h2><p><strong>Rule:</strong> Put <strong>か分かりません</strong> after a <strong>plain-form question clause</strong>, often one containing a question word, to say what you <strong>do not know</strong>. In English, this is usually &quot;I don&#39;t know where / when / which …&quot;.</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Before か分かりません</th><th>Pattern</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Verb</td><td>Plain form ＋ か分かりません</td></tr><tr><td>い-adjective</td><td>Plain form ＋ か分かりません</td></tr><tr><td>な-adjective</td><td>Adjective stem ＋ か分かりません</td></tr><tr><td>Noun</td><td>Noun ＋ か分かりません</td></tr></tbody></table><p><strong>Note:</strong> Do not put <strong>だ</strong> between a noun or な-adjective and <strong>か</strong>: use <strong>N か</strong> and <strong>な-adjective stem ＋ か</strong>, not <em>N だか</em> or <em>な-adjective だか</em>.</p><ul><li>新幹線の切符をどこで買うか分かりません。 — I don&#39;t know where to buy a Shinkansen ticket.</li><li>いつ雨が降るか分かりません。 — I don&#39;t know when it will rain.</li><li>中村さんの傘はどれか分かりません。 — I don&#39;t know which one is Nakamura&#39;s umbrella.</li></ul><h2 id="ずつ-each-little-by-little" tabindex="-1">ずつ（each / little by little） <a class="header-anchor" href="#ずつ-each-little-by-little" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;ずつ（each / little by little）&quot;">​</a></h2><p><strong>Rule:</strong> Put <strong>ずつ</strong> after a <strong>quantity</strong> or a <strong>degree word</strong> to show that an action happens repeatedly in the stated amount, or that the same amount is allotted to each person or item. Depending on context, it means &quot;… at a time,&quot; &quot;… each,&quot; or &quot;little by little.&quot;</p><ul><li>１人ずつ発言する。 — Each person speaks in turn.</li><li>日本語が少しずつ上手になりました。 — My Japanese improved little by little.</li><li>みかんを一人３個ずつあげます。 — I&#39;ll give each person three mandarins.</li></ul><h2 id="n-にする-choose-decide-on" tabindex="-1">N にする（choose / decide on） <a class="header-anchor" href="#n-にする-choose-decide-on" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;N にする（choose / decide on）&quot;">​</a></h2><p><strong>Rule:</strong> Put <strong>にする</strong> after a noun to say that you <strong>choose or decide on</strong> that option from several possibilities. It is especially common when ordering food or drinks and when shopping.</p><ul><li>A：お飲み物はどうなさいますか。 — What would you like to drink?</li><li>B：ビールにします。 — I&#39;ll have a beer.</li><li>どの色がいいかわからなかったから、一色ずつにしました。 — I couldn&#39;t decide which color was best, so I chose one of each color.</li><li>A：今日のお昼は何にしましょうか。 — What shall we have for lunch today?</li><li>B：私はラーメンが食べたいです。ラーメンにしましょうよ。 — I&#39;d like ramen. Let&#39;s have ramen.</li></ul><h2 id="adverbial-forms-of-adjectives-形容詞連用形" tabindex="-1">Adverbial forms of adjectives（形容詞連用形） <a class="header-anchor" href="#adverbial-forms-of-adjectives-形容詞連用形" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Adverbial forms of adjectives（形容詞連用形）&quot;">​</a></h2><p><strong>Rule:</strong> An adjective can modify a verb to describe <strong>how an action is performed</strong> or the state in which it is done. Change an <strong>い-adjective</strong> from <strong>～い</strong> to <strong>～く</strong> before the verb. Add <strong>に</strong> to the stem of a <strong>な-adjective</strong> before the verb.</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Adjective type</th><th>Pattern</th><th>Example</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>い-adjective</td><td>Drop い ＋ く ＋ verb</td><td>早い → 早く走る</td></tr><tr><td>な-adjective</td><td>Stem ＋ に ＋ verb</td><td>親切 → 親切に答える</td></tr></tbody></table><ul><li>皆さんからの質問に親切に答えます。 — I answer everyone&#39;s questions kindly.</li><li>お皿をきれいに洗いました。 — I washed the dishes thoroughly.</li><li>早く走りましょう。 — Let&#39;s run quickly.</li></ul><h2 id="ので-cause-and-reason-ii-verbs-and-adjectives" tabindex="-1">～ので（cause and reason II: verbs and adjectives） <a class="header-anchor" href="#ので-cause-and-reason-ii-verbs-and-adjectives" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;～ので（cause and reason II: verbs and adjectives）&quot;">​</a></h2><p><strong>Rule:</strong> This extends the earlier <a href="#reason-node-noun">N なので（cause and reason II）</a> rule to <strong>verbs and adjectives</strong>. <strong>ので</strong> follows a clause giving the reason for what comes next, often presenting it as a matter of circumstance. It can also end a sentence when the result is clear from context. Compare the earlier <a href="#reason-kara">～から（reason）</a> rule.</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Predicate type</th><th>Form before ので</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Verb</td><td>Plain form ＋ ので</td></tr><tr><td>い-adjective</td><td>Plain form ＋ ので</td></tr><tr><td>な-adjective</td><td>Stem ＋ な ＋ ので</td></tr><tr><td>Noun</td><td>Noun ＋ な ＋ ので</td></tr></tbody></table><p>For a non-past affirmative noun or な-adjective, use <strong>な</strong> before <strong>ので</strong>, rather than <strong>だ</strong>: <strong>N なので</strong>, <strong>な-adjective stem ＋ なので</strong>.</p><ul><li>頭が痛いので、早く帰りたいです。 — I have a headache, so I&#39;d like to go home early.</li><li>海外にいるので、参加ができません。 — Since I&#39;m overseas, I can&#39;t participate.</li><li>発音があまりきれいじゃないので、たくさん練習したいです。 — My pronunciation isn&#39;t very good, so I want to practice a lot.</li><li>ここは、今人気のお店なので、予約が難しいです。 — This place is popular now, so it&#39;s difficult to get a reservation.</li></ul><h2 id="v-て形-te-form" tabindex="-1">V て形（te-form） <a class="header-anchor" href="#v-て形-te-form" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;V て形（te-form）&quot;">​</a></h2><p><strong>Rule:</strong> The <strong>te-form</strong> links a verb to what follows (and, then, please …, is …ing). It is built the same way as the plain past <strong>V た</strong>: replace <strong>て</strong> with <strong>た</strong> and <strong>で</strong> with <strong>だ</strong>. How you form it depends on the verb class and, for Class I, on the final kana. In Japanese school grammar the te-form is called the <strong>second continuative form</strong>（动词第二连用形）.</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Class</th><th>Ending</th><th>Te-form</th><th>Examples</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Class II（一段動詞）</td><td>る</td><td>drop る ＋ <strong>て</strong></td><td>起きる → 起きて、食べる → 食べて、見る → 見て</td></tr><tr><td>Class III（サ変・カ変）</td><td>—</td><td>する → し ＋ て；来る（くる） → 来（き） ＋ て</td><td>する → <strong>して</strong>、勉強する → <strong>勉強して</strong>、来る → <strong>来て</strong>（きて）</td></tr><tr><td>Class I（五段動詞）</td><td>う・つ・る</td><td>→ っ ＋ <strong>て</strong></td><td>使う → 使って、待つ → 待って、送る → 送って、買う → 買って、帰る → 帰って</td></tr><tr><td></td><td>む・ぶ・ぬ</td><td>→ ん ＋ <strong>で</strong></td><td>飲む → 飲んで、呼ぶ → 呼んで、死ぬ → 死んで、読む → 読んで、遊ぶ → 遊んで</td></tr><tr><td></td><td>く</td><td>→ い ＋ <strong>て</strong></td><td>書く → 書いて、聞く → 聞いて、歩く → 歩いて</td></tr><tr><td></td><td>ぐ</td><td>→ い ＋ <strong>で</strong></td><td>泳ぐ → 泳いで、急ぐ → 急いで、脱ぐ → 脱いで</td></tr><tr><td></td><td>す</td><td>→ し ＋ <strong>て</strong></td><td>話す → 話して、出す → 出して</td></tr></tbody></table><p><strong>Exception:</strong> <strong>行く → 行って</strong>（行った）, not 行いて.</p><p><strong>Class I in four steps (textbook order):</strong> change only the last kana, then add て／で.</p><ol><li><strong>う・つ・る → っ ＋ て</strong>: つかう → つかって、まつ → まって、おくる → おくって</li><li><strong>く → い ＋ て</strong>: かく → かいて. <strong>ぐ → い ＋ で</strong>: およぐ → およいで</li><li><strong>す → し ＋ て</strong>: はなす → はなして</li><li><strong>む・ぶ・ぬ → ん ＋ で</strong>: のむ → のんで、よぶ → よんで、しぬ → しんで</li></ol><p><strong>Tip:</strong> <strong>で</strong> appears only when the original ending is voiced: <strong>ぐ, む, ぶ, ぬ</strong>. Verbs ending in <strong>く</strong> or <strong>す</strong>, and the irregular <strong>来る／する</strong>, always take <strong>て</strong>: 聞く → 聞いて（聞いた）, never 聞いで. 帰る looks like Class II, but it is Class I, so it becomes 帰って.</p><ul><li>手紙を書いて、ポストに入れました。 — I wrote a letter and put it in the postbox.</li><li>友達に道を聞いて、駅まで歩きました。 — I asked a friend the way and walked to the station.</li><li>昨日、友達が家に来て、一緒に勉強しました。 — Yesterday a friend came over and we studied together.</li><li>海で泳いで、本を読んで、早く寝ました。 — I swam in the sea, read a book and went to bed early.</li></ul><h2 id="v-ている-action-in-progress-ている-i" tabindex="-1">V ている（action in progress, ている I） <a class="header-anchor" href="#v-ている-action-in-progress-ている-i" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;V ている（action in progress, ている I）&quot;">​</a></h2><div class="important custom-block github-alert"><p class="custom-block-title">IMPORTANT</p><p></p><p>A core pattern with many uses. In this lesson, <strong>V て ＋ いる</strong> means an action is <strong>in progress right now</strong> (&quot;is …ing&quot;, Chinese 正在……). It often comes with <strong>今</strong> (now) or <strong>ちょうど</strong> (just, right now). <strong>ている</strong> is the plain form; the polite form is <strong>ています</strong>.</p></div><p><strong>Rule:</strong> Take the te-form of the verb (see V て形 above) and add <strong>いる</strong>. <strong>いる</strong> is a Class II verb, so it conjugates like 見る. In casual speech the い is often dropped: 弾いてる, 考えてる.</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th></th><th>Plain（简体）</th><th>Polite（敬体）</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Present</td><td>弾いて<strong>いる</strong> — is playing</td><td>弾いて<strong>います</strong> — is playing</td></tr><tr><td>Past</td><td>弾いて<strong>いた</strong> — was playing</td><td>弾いて<strong>いました</strong> — was playing</td></tr><tr><td>Negative</td><td>弾いて<strong>いない</strong> — is not playing</td><td>弾いて<strong>いません</strong> — is not playing</td></tr><tr><td>Past negative</td><td>弾いて<strong>いなかった</strong> — was not playing</td><td>弾いて<strong>いませんでした</strong> — was not playing</td></tr><tr><td>Question</td><td>弾いて<strong>いる？</strong></td><td>弾いて<strong>いますか</strong></td></tr></tbody></table><p><strong>Tip:</strong> Only <strong>いる</strong> changes. The te-form part stays as it is: 読んでいる → 読んでいた／読んでいない. Questions with <strong>何をしていますか</strong> ask &quot;What are you doing (now)?&quot;.</p><ul><li>A：今、何をしていますか。B：ピアノを弾いています。 — A: What are you doing now? B: I&#39;m playing the piano.</li><li>A：すみません。この問題がわからないのですが。B：実は、わたしもこの問題について、ちょうど考えているんですよ。一緒に考えましょう。 — A: Excuse me, I don&#39;t understand this problem. B: Actually, I&#39;m just thinking about it too. Let&#39;s work it out together.</li><li>今、運転しながら、音楽を聴いています。 — Right now I&#39;m listening to music while driving.</li><li>昨日の夜八時ごろ、テレビを見ていました。 — Around eight last night, I was watching TV.</li><li>今は雨が降っていません。 — It isn&#39;t raining now.</li><li>電話したとき、兄は寝ていませんでした。 — When I called, my older brother wasn&#39;t sleeping.</li></ul><h2 id="v-た-plain-past" tabindex="-1">V た（plain past） <a class="header-anchor" href="#v-た-plain-past" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;V た（plain past）&quot;">​</a></h2><p><strong>Rule:</strong> <strong>V た</strong> marks a completed action or the past. It is the plain form of <strong>V ました</strong>. It is built exactly like the te-form (see V て形 above): just change <strong>て → た</strong> and <strong>で → だ</strong>.</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Dictionary form</th><th>Te-form</th><th>た-form</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>読む</td><td>読んで</td><td>読ん<strong>だ</strong></td></tr><tr><td>聞く</td><td>聞いて</td><td>聞い<strong>た</strong></td></tr><tr><td>食べる</td><td>食べて</td><td>食べ<strong>た</strong></td></tr><tr><td>勉強する</td><td>勉強して</td><td>勉強し<strong>た</strong></td></tr></tbody></table><p><strong>Tip:</strong> The 行く exception carries over: 行って → <strong>行った</strong>. The plain past negative is ～なかった, for example 見つからない → 見つから<strong>なかった</strong>.</p><ul><li>漢字の読み方を間違えた。 — I got the reading of the kanji wrong.</li><li>鈴木さんはスマホをどこかに落とした。 — Suzuki dropped his phone somewhere.</li><li>あっちこっち探したが、見つからなかった。 — I searched all over, but couldn&#39;t find it.</li></ul><h2 id="v-て、v-て、v-ます-linking-actions-in-sequence" tabindex="-1">V て、V て、V ます（linking actions in sequence） <a class="header-anchor" href="#v-て、v-て、v-ます-linking-actions-in-sequence" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;V て、V て、V ます（linking actions in sequence）&quot;">​</a></h2><p><strong>Rule:</strong> When one sentence has two or more verbs in a row, link them with the te-form <strong>V て</strong>. The actions happen <strong>in the order given</strong>. Only the <strong>last</strong> verb shows the tense and politeness (ます／ました／たいです). English uses &quot;and (then)&quot;, and Chinese usually leaves the sequence untranslated.</p><p><strong>Tip:</strong> Whether the whole chain is past or present depends only on the final verb: 起きて、勉強しました is past, 起きて、行きます is present or future.</p><ul><li>3時に起きて、勉強しました。 — I got up at three and studied.</li><li>私は東京に行って、東京タワーとスカイツリーを見たいです。 — I want to go to Tokyo and see Tokyo Tower and the Skytree.</li><li>インターネットで資料を調べて、パワーポイントを作ります。 — I&#39;ll look up material online and make a PowerPoint.</li><li>朝起きて、シャワーを浴びて、会社へ行きます。 — In the morning I get up, take a shower and go to work.</li></ul><h2 id="v-てください-request" tabindex="-1">V てください（request） <a class="header-anchor" href="#v-てください-request" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;V てください（request）&quot;">​</a></h2><p><strong>Rule:</strong> Te-form ＋ <strong>ください</strong> asks the listener to do something (&quot;please …&quot;, Chinese 请……). Japanese has many polite ways to make a request, and <strong>V てください</strong> is mostly a polite <strong>instruction or request</strong>. Because it still sounds like telling someone what to do, it is <strong>generally not used toward superiors</strong>.</p><ul><li>今日はお疲れさまでした。ゆっくり休んでください。 — Thanks for your hard work today. Please get some good rest.</li><li>電話番号を教えてください。 — Please tell me your phone number.</li><li>明日の10時半に来てください。 — Please come at 10:30 tomorrow.</li><li>これは中国のお土産です。どうぞ、食べてください。 — This is a souvenir from China. Please have some.</li></ul><h2 id="つもりだ-intention" tabindex="-1">～つもりだ（intention） <a class="header-anchor" href="#つもりだ-intention" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;～つもりだ（intention）&quot;">​</a></h2><p><strong>Form:</strong> <strong>V る ＋ つもりだ</strong> / <strong>V ない ＋ つもりだ</strong>（polite: つもりです）</p><p><strong>Rule:</strong> The dictionary form or ない-form plus <strong>つもりだ</strong> states the speaker&#39;s <strong>intention or plan</strong> (&quot;I plan to…&quot;, &quot;I intend to…&quot;, Chinese （我）打算……). <strong>V るつもりだ</strong> is fairly strict about person and is generally used <strong>only in the first person</strong>. Asking someone about their plans with a <strong>つもりだ</strong> question can sound <strong>rude</strong> to the listener, so avoid it except between people who are close (see the third example).</p><p><strong>Not planning to:</strong> for &quot;I don&#39;t plan to…&quot; or &quot;I have no intention of…&quot;, use the <strong>ない-form ＋ つもりだ</strong>.</p><ul><li>この夏休みはノーベル賞の受賞作品を読むつもりです。 — This summer vacation I plan to read Nobel Prize–winning works.</li><li>明日からお酒をやめるつもりです。 — I plan to quit drinking starting tomorrow.</li><li>ジョージさん、レポートは何について書くつもりですか。 — George, what are you planning to write your report about?（OK only between close friends）</li><li>この冬休みは帰国しないつもりです。 — I don&#39;t plan to go back to my home country this winter vacation.</li></ul><h2 id="予定だ-plan-schedule" tabindex="-1">～予定だ（plan / schedule） <a class="header-anchor" href="#予定だ-plan-schedule" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;～予定だ（plan / schedule）&quot;">​</a></h2><p><strong>Form:</strong> <strong>V る ＋ 予定だ</strong> / <strong>N の ＋ 予定だ</strong>（polite: 予定です）</p><p><strong>Rule:</strong> <strong>予定だ</strong> states a plan that is <strong>already more or less settled</strong> (&quot;is scheduled to…&quot;, &quot;plans to…&quot;, Chinese 计划……、准备……). Compared with <strong>～つもりだ</strong>, it is <strong>objective</strong>: it reports the schedule without the speaker&#39;s personal will. So it is <strong>not limited to the first person</strong>, and the tone is softer.</p><p><strong>Tip:</strong> つもりだ is what I intend to do; 予定だ is what&#39;s on the schedule.</p><ul><li>李さんはジョージさんと一緒に村上春樹先生の講演会に参加する予定です。 — Li is planning to attend Haruki Murakami&#39;s lecture with George.</li><li>今週の土曜日、歌舞伎を見に行く予定です。 — I&#39;m planning to go see kabuki this Saturday.</li><li>今回のプロジェクトは2週間の予定です。 — This project is scheduled to take two weeks.</li><li>明日は朝6時の飛行機に乗る予定です。 — Tomorrow I&#39;m scheduled to take the 6 a.m. flight.</li></ul><h2 id="中-ちゅう-in-the-middle-of" tabindex="-1">～中（ちゅう: in the middle of） <a class="header-anchor" href="#中-ちゅう-in-the-middle-of" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;～中（ちゅう: in the middle of）&quot;">​</a></h2><p><strong>Rule:</strong> <strong>中</strong>, read <strong>ちゅう</strong>, attaches mostly to Sino-Japanese nouns that carry an action meaning (漢語). It shows that the action is <strong>in progress / under way</strong> (&quot;in the middle of…&quot;, &quot;currently…&quot;). Common examples are <strong>営業中</strong>, open for business; <strong>準備中</strong>, in preparation; and <strong>会議中</strong>, in a meeting.</p><ul><li>A：近くのレストランは、今、やっていますか。B：はい、今営業中ですよ。 — A: Is the restaurant nearby open now? B: Yes, it&#39;s open right now.</li><li>会議中に、お邪魔してすみません。 — Sorry to interrupt you during the meeting.</li><li>隣が工事中で、とてもうるさいです。 — There&#39;s construction going on next door, so it&#39;s very noisy.</li></ul>',420)])])}const p=e(s,[["render",r]]);export{c as __pageData,p as default};
